-   "state_note": "[2026-09-19 06:2x] #541 IN WORK (e8a2bc): the fix (member 4 + a per-CPU latch) unchanged since pass 1; the detector rebuilt from pass 2's findings (22 rows, two sessions); re-table running (regress/artifacts/alphavmkill/killtable2_20260919/); the commit is HELD for a first-hand fable5 ruling (both 'fable5' answers so far were opus-4-8 after refusals -- see agentrefusal).  FILED 2026-09-07, found while checking [[alphapfnoffset]]'s reachability rather than by any seat.\n\n    if (vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000ULL) {\n            fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**A guest-chosen virtual address ends the emulator process.**  It sits inside the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap arm,\nso it is reached BEFORE virtual memory is configured -- i.e. early in a boot, on an address the kernel\nchooses, not one a user program supplies.\n\nIt is the same de-fatalisation class this project has worked through repeatedly in devices, arriving in a CPU\nmemory path where nobody has been looking.  **The disposition is NOT obvious, which is why this is filed\nrather than fixed:** declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation and it will fault or hang somewhere\nless legible, so this may be one of the rare sites where a loud stop is genuinely better than continuing --\nand if so the round's product is a RECORD saying so, plus a latch, not a removal.\n\nMED: reachable by construction, but only on a machine whose Alpha VM support is already declared incomplete\nseven TODOs over.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : replace the terminal path with the architecturally appropriate outcome\n    one oracle     : that outcome is observed for the affected address class\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** grok ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- the sixth and last Alpha row.  THE CLUSTER IS NOW FULLY ASSESSED. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE BUT NARROWER THAN FILED: it holds for kernel mode with PTBR == 0 on that exact\nVA.**  There are TWO hits on `0xfffffffd80000000` in `memory_alpha.c` and **only one terminates**;\nthe other is a bare `fatal(\"AYONA3\n\")` -- a debug string left in the tree.  `fatal()` only prints;\nthe termination is the `exit(1)` immediately after, with nothing recovering in between.\n\n## THE CENSUS I ASKED FOR, and it changes the framing\n\n| walker | unmodelled / stub region | ordinary translation miss |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alpha | this one VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)`; other PTBR==0 VAs **ACCEPT** identity | `fatal` + **`return 0`**, no guest exception (`#194` removed the abort here) |\n| MIPS | -- | `mips_cpu_exception`, `return 0` |\n| ARM | unimplemented descriptor type: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `arm_exception`, `return 0` |\n| PPC | 64-bit translation: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `ppc_exception`, `return 0` |\n| M88K | internal inconsistency: `exit(1)` | `m88k_exception`, `return 0` |\n| SH | unimplemented kernel VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)` (**honours FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS**) | `sh_exception`, `return 0` |\n\n**The kill is an outlier in BOTH in-file directions** -- it is neither the PTBR==0 siblings' ACCEPT\nnor the walk's own `return 0`, which this project already chose over host death in `#194`.\n\n## *** ACCEPT IS REFUTED ON A PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE SHARPEST PART OF THE ANSWER ***\n\nDeleting the `exit(1)` looks like member 4, because `*return_paddr` is already computed above it.\n**But that value is the comment's own \"UGLY hack\": a K0SEG mask applied to a K1SEG address**, giving\na physical address near 4 TB against a default 128 MB of RAM.  Data then takes the unmapped-physical\npath -- **zeros returned, writes dropped, and the access reported SUCCESSFUL.**\n\n> *\"That is the silent bogus map the landmine exists to refuse.\"*\n\n**So a bare de-fatalisation here would be WORSE than the kill it removes**, which is the opposite of\nthis queue's usual shape and is exactly the kind of thing the option-set rule exists to surface.\n\n## DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, `return 0`\n\n* **5 FAULT is architecturally right and NOT AVAILABLE.**  Choosing it obliges building delivery\n  first: a PAL frame, `kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a\n  redirected PC.  **That is a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.**  *My brief's caution\n  that FAULT may be unavailable on Alpha is confirmed, with the bill itemised.*\n* **7 IMPLEMENT does not apply** -- its tell is \"no predicate, whole path stubbed\"; this is a\n  one-address predicate, and K1SEG translation IS the walk once PTBR is set.\n* **2 silent DECLINE drops the only signal the author planted.**\n\n## SCOPE: STILL SIX SEPARATE ROUNDS\n\nI asked whether the shared missing MM-dispatch now meets codex's bar for merging this with\n`alphafaultclaim`.  **It does not:** *\"missing MM-dispatch is a CARRIER, not a shared repair.\"*  A\ncommon blocker is not one fix design and one oracle.\n\n## SEVERITY: **med**, explicitly BELOW `alphatbinegsel`\n\nRaised from unset to `med` on grok's argument: this needs a guest already executing privileged code\nwith PTBR == 0 at one specific address, whereas `alphatbinegsel` is a documented primitive silently\nunder-flushing during ordinary operation.  *The ranking is the seat's and I am adopting it rather\nthan substituting my own.*",
