-   "state_note": "[2026-09-19 06:2x] #544 IN WORK (e8a2bc): the fix is drafted with 20 launcher controls and a 38-row / 21-variant tally self-test; pass 2 in flight on panel_20260919_060945.  Built 2026-09-07 after the `lcaconfwidth` census was REFUSED by codex's content filter and my\nlauncher reported it as a completed run.  **Three defects, all mine, all found within an hour of\neach other, and all of one family: a check that answers the same whether or not the thing it tests\nis true.**\n\n1. **The refusal check read the wrong stream.**  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout\n   and puts `ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.` on STDERR.  The check\n   grepped stdout and printed `refusal hits: 0` on a refusal that had already destroyed 108,432\n   tokens of completed work.\n2. **The script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status, printed it, and ended\n   on a successful command, so the harness reported the task COMPLETED.  Failure laundered into a\n   clean completion -- the fourth instance of that class recorded here.\n3. **The FIX introduced a third defect, measured on its first real run.**  Grepping stderr freely\n   for wall text reported `wall: 3` on a HEALTHY seat, because **codex streams the source it reads\n   into stderr** -- and all three hits were this project's own ledger prose about seat walls, one of\n   them the line *\"whose quotation of the pattern MATCHES\"*.  That is the carrier's\n   quoting-a-machine-read-marker rule, landing on a tool written by someone who had just read it.\n\n> **THE DISCRIMINATOR: codex's OWN diagnostics are `ERROR:` at line start; everything else on stderr\n> is echoed content.**  Anchor stderr tests on `^ERROR:`; grep stdout freely.  Exit 7 refusal, 8\n> wall, 6 empty-with-neither, else `rc`.\n\n**Controlled in four states**, and (b) is the one that matters: a stderr line containing *usage\nlimit* inside echoed source must NOT trip the wall check.\n\n    a  ERROR: flagged...           -> REFUSED   (want REFUSED)\n    b  echoed source w/ 'usage limit', good stdout -> answered  (want answered)\n    c  ERROR: usage limit          -> WALLED    (want WALLED)\n    d  ordinary reasoning, good stdout          -> answered  (want answered)\n\n*** THE CLAIM I ORIGINALLY CLOSED THIS ROW WITH WAS FALSE, AND I MADE IT IN A ROW ABOUT CHECKS THAT\nDO NOT CHECK.  Corrected within the hour, by reading `panel.sh` instead of assuming. ***\n\nI wrote that *\"`panel.sh` fires the same seat with its own check and has NOT been given this\ndiscriminator.\"*  **It has had one longer than mine, and a better one.**  Its `check()` anchors error\nsentinels to the **first 400 bytes** and its wall test to the **last 400 bytes**, with the reason in\nits own comment: *\"a review that merely quotes 'was retired' or 'usage:' gets mis-flagged (it did, on\nthe first live run).\"*\n\n**So the tree already held the lesson and I re-learned it by breaking it.**  The two discriminators\ndiffer and both are sound:\n\n    panel.sh    POSITION -- head/tail 400 bytes.  Transport-agnostic; works for any seat whose\n                own status lands at the edges of the file, which is all of them.\n    cxlaunch.sh FORMAT   -- codex's `^ERROR:` lines.  Sharper for codex, useless for the others.\n\n*The honest ordering: `panel.sh`'s is the general one and mine is a codex-specific refinement.  A\nfuture consolidation should take POSITION as the base test and add FORMAT where a seat's own error\nprefix is known -- not the other way round.*\n\n*Kept open for that consolidation, not for the phantom gap I invented.*\n\n*** A SECOND SHAPE OF THE SAME CLASS, MEASURED ON MYSELF 2026-09-08: THE HARNESS REPORTED\n\"completed (exit code 0)\" FOR A codex RUN THAT NEVER STARTED. ***\n\nA background launch used a RELATIVE artifacts path.  The background shell's cwd was not\n`GXEMUL-SEC`, so both halves failed:\n\n    cat: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/Q3_brief.md: No such file or directory\n    bash: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_q3_20260908.txt: No such file or directory\n    EXIT=1\n\nand the task notification said **completed (exit code 0)** -- the WRAPPER's status, not the command's.\n`codex` was never invoked; no `.txt` and no `.err` were created at all.\n\n**The tell is the carrier's own three-state rule, and it is the state people forget:** log MISSING =\nnever started; log 0 bytes = started, mid-run or dead; log growing = alive.  A REFUSED codex run\nleaves a 0-byte `.txt` AND a large `.err`.  **This left neither** -- which is how it is distinguished\nfrom a refusal, and why a check that only looks for the refusal text would have called it a clean run.\n\nTwo things follow, and the second is the cheap one:\n* **an explicit `echo \"EXIT=$?\"` after a backgrounded seat launch is what caught it.**  Without it\n  the missing answer would have looked like a refusal or a wall.\n* **use ABSOLUTE paths for every seat launch.**  The carrier already says the session cwd DRIFTS and\n  mandates absolute paths for `panel.sh`; the same applies to any hand-rolled seat invocation, and a\n  BACKGROUND shell does not reliably inherit the foreground `cd` at all."
+   "state_note": "[2026-09-21 16:2x] #544 IN WORK (e8a2bc).  PASS 5 (panel_20260921_154943, 7 answered, the kimi CLI walled mid-answer): the chain does NOT close, and the finding is MEASURED ON A REAL FILE.  agy and grok independently found that **a wall notice can span more than one line**: grok's 402 wall is a pretty-printed JSON object whose column-0 marker line carries NO strong phrase (it is on the `message' line below) with twenty-odd lines after it.  Measured on panel_20260824_191634/grok.txt: `_wall_at_end' returned FALSE.  The four real files score walled only because no verdict parses -- one that HAD streamed a verdict would have been CREDITED.  My pass-4 class closure enumerated distance, trailers, case, colour and quote forms and never asked whether a notice is one line or several: a MISSING CELL, and the third consecutive pass to find a defect in this one test.  v12 folds a marker line with its structured continuation into one notice unit (bounded; a column-0 prose line ends the fold, so a quoting answer stays credited -- both directions rowed, R35/R36).  MEASURED: the real grok file now reads WALLED; selftest 64 rows / 49 mutants, every mutant red; sweep over 401 dirs changes ZERO reports against v11 on quiescent files.  The selftest also caught my own edit breaking mutant R32's anchor (MUTANT TEXT NOT FOUND, not a quiet pass) -- re-aimed.  DISCLOSED rather than fixed: a ONE-LINE refusal after a verdict (deepseek), because the two-line rule is warranted by 20 of 20 real refusals and catching it trades a false DOWN."
