-   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  Surfaced while verifying a claim in the grok seat's `lcabadval` pass-1 BODY (its\nverdict line did not carry it) -- and **verified to be sharper than the seat stated it**, by opening\nboth functions.\n\n**THE DEFECT: an ACCEPTED write to one register arms a host kill in a DIFFERENT function.**\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)`, `case LCA_IOC_CONF`:\n\n    d->ioc_conf = idata;\n    /*  Only bit 0 is implemented so far, the PCI bus 0 vs\n        bus non-0 selection bit.  */\n    if (idata & ~1) {\n            fatal(\"TODO: Write to unimplemented bit of IOC_CONF: 0x%\" PRIx64 \"\\n\", ...);\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**`0x1` PASSES that guard.**  `idata & ~1` is 0, so the write is accepted, stored, and the arm's own\ncomment describes bit 0 as the implemented selector.\n\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` then consumes it:\n\n    bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    if (bus == 0) {\n            for (dev=0; dev<21; dev++)\n                    if (tag & (0x800 << dev))\n                            break;\n            if (dev >= 21) {\n                    /*  fatal(\"[ LCA: No bus 0 device? TODO ]\\n\");\n                    exit(1);  */\n                    dev = 0;\n            }\n    } else {\n            fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\nSo: **guest writes 1 to IOC_CONF (accepted), then performs any PCI configuration-space access, and\nthe host process exits.**  Two guest accesses, both to registers the model implements, neither of\nthem rejected.\n\n*** WHY IT IS ITS OWN ROW AND NOT PART OF [[lcabadval]]. ***  Every kill `lcabadval` covers is a\nguard REFUSING a value it does not recognise.  **This one fires on a value the guard ACCEPTS**, in a\ndifferent function, on a later access.  The acceptance and the kill are separated in both code and\ntime, which is why a per-arm audit of `lca_ioc` walks straight past it -- as mine did, twice.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT'S FRAMING NEEDED CORRECTING, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH RECORDING. ***  grok wrote\nthat this exit becomes *\"newly reachable from a CONF extra-bit write that also sets bit 0; today\nthat write dies in the CONF guard first.\"*  That is true of an EXTRA-BIT write (e.g. `0x3`), and it\nmade the kill sound like a CONSEQUENCE of adopting option A.  **It is not: `0x1` alone reaches it\ntoday, with no fix applied.**  A widens which CONF values arrive here; it does not open the door.\nThe door is open now.  *Checked by reading both arms rather than by accepting the seat's sentence --\nwhich is this project's own rule, and the seat was right about the site while being wrong about the\ntiming.*\n\n**THE IRONY, and it is a design signal rather than a joke:** the `bus == 0` branch is the file's own\ngraceful-degradation PRECEDENT -- a commented-out `fatal()`/`exit(1)` with `dev = 0;` substituted --\nand [[lcabadval]] cites that very loop as a transferable in-tree warrant.  **The `else` five lines\nbelow it is a bare kill.**  Same function, opposite dispositions, no comment explaining why.\n\n**REACHABILITY, stated honestly rather than assumed.**  Bit 0 is a bus SELECTOR, so a guest reaches\nthis by selecting a bus the model does not implement and then doing config access -- plausible for\nfirmware or an OS that probes beyond bus 0, and trivially reachable by a probe.  **Whether any\nshipped guest on `alphabook1` actually does it is NOT established here**, and the row does not claim\nit.  What is established is that no guard stands between an accepted write and the kill.\n\n**WITNESS: a rung-3 probe is available and most of it already exists.**  `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`\nis `ST_ALPHABOOK1` ONLY (`machine_alpha.c`), and `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py` ALREADY drives a\nguest `stq`/`ldq` at `LCA_IOC_CONF` (`0xfffffc0180000020`, K0SEG | physical) on unmodified\n`alphabook1`.  This row needs one more session: `stq 0x1` to CONF, then a PCI config-space access.\n**Three traps that probe already paid for:** debugger `dump` skips `DM_DEFAULT` handlers; a debugger\n`put d` of a killing value IS a host kill but is NOT a guest instruction, so it does not witness; and\nHAE at window offset 0 silently succeeds, so a miss control must not use `LCA_IOC_BASE+0`.\n\n**DISPOSITION, not yet adjudicated:** it is the same de-fatalisation family, but the option set\ndiffers because the kill is not a value refusal -- the honest members here are DECLINE THE CONFIG\nACCESS (return without dispatching, with a latched announce) or IMPLEMENT bus != 0.  **Note the\narchitecturally correct error path is blocked for the same reason grok gave on the sibling row:\n`LCA_IOC_STAT0`/`STAT1` have no case at all and hit the `default:` arm's `exit(1)`, so LCA cannot\nreport a device error through the register the header designates for it.**\n\nsev HIGH: a guest-drivable host kill, reached by two ordinary accesses with no rejected value\nanywhere on the path.\n\n*** SIBLING CHECK DISCHARGED WITH A DENOMINATOR, 2026-09-07 -- the class has EXACTLY ONE member in\nthis file. ***  The rule is grep for the siblings before calling a finding whole; here the question\nis which lca_ioc fields have a consumer at all, since a field nothing reads cannot arm a delayed\nkill.  Counting references that are neither the readback (`odata = d->X`) nor the store (`d->X = `):\n\n    ioc_conf          1 consumer   <- `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` in lca_pci_conf.  THIS ROW.\n    tlb_enable        0\n    window_base_0     0            window_base_1     0\n    window_mask_0     0            window_mask_1     0\n    window_t_base_0   0            window_t_base_1   0\n\n**So the accepted-write-arms-a-later-kill shape cannot recur elsewhere in dev_lca.c**: the other\nseven fields are write-and-read-back with nothing downstream.  That is also why [[lcabadval]]s five\npure-storage arms are a different problem from this one, and why a fix here does not generalise to\nthem.\n\n*Not claimed beyond this file.  The general class -- an ACCEPTED store that a later access consumes\ninto a terminating path -- is a plausible cross-device census and is NOT run here.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS-DESIGN CHECK WAS REFUSED BY codex ON A CONTENT FILTER, 2026-09-07 -- recorded as a\nSEAT FAILURE, never as agreement. ***  Zero-byte `.txt`, exit 0, the error only in the `.err`:\n*\"This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.\"*  Full analysis and the two same-day\nnegative controls are on [[codexsubjectrefusal]].  **Re-fired on grok**, which reads the tree, takes\nits brief from a file and has no such filter -- so the question is covered, not lost.  The brief\nitself is preserved at `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/WITNESS_brief.md` so the trigger can be\nstudied rather than guessed at.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN IS SOUND -- grok, 2026-09-07, ON THE BRIEF codex REFUSED.  The\nalternate-transport rule paid off completely: the recovery is BETTER than the refused seat would\nhave given, not a degraded substitute. ***\n\n**DENOMINATOR, and it is the largest this row has seen:** all of `dev_lca.c` (390 lines -- both\nhandlers, `DEVINIT`, ISA, interrupt stubs); `DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_pci_conf)` and\n`DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc)` end to end; `bus_pci_data_access`/`bus_pci_setaddr` in `bus_pci.c`;\n`alpha_translate_v2p` K0SEG in `memory_alpha.c`; the device and unmapped paths in `memory_rw.c`;\n`LS_GENERIC_N`/`LS_N` in `cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c`; `fatal()` in `debugmsg.c`; `ST_ALPHABOOK1`\nin `machine_alpha.c`; and all of `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`.\n\n**CONFIRMED:** S2 reaches `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` for ANY access in the window -- no `switch` on\n`relative_addr`, no `len` test, no `writeflag` test that can skip it, no early `return`; the only\n`writeflag` use before it is filling `idata`, which is not a guard.  `len` is first used AFTER the\nkill.  And `ioc_conf` has **exactly two readers**, so nothing else can be armed by S1.\n\n*** FOUR CORRECTIONS THAT CHANGE THE CONSTRUCTION: ***\n\n1. **MY LIVENESS CONTROL IS MISLABELLED.**  `ioc rt` proves the WRITER (`lca_ioc`'s CONF arm)\n   round-trips `0x1`.  **It says nothing about S2's window.**  PCI_CONF liveness is the armed\n   session's unique message (the handler ran) PLUS the control surviving (the same access is not an\n   unconditional kill).  A positive signature is available if wanted: an empty-slot read returns\n   `0xffffffffffffffff`, where unmapped returns 0.\n2. **S2 MUST BE A READ, NOT A WRITE.**  Both reach the kill, but an empty-slot WRITE makes\n   `bus_pci_data_access` print `[ ... write to non-existant device ... ]` -- so the CONTROL would\n   carry a second `fatal()` on stderr.  An empty-slot READ of reg 0 is the architectural ID probe\n   and is silent.\n3. **A TIGHTER VARIANT: ONE PROCESS, not two sessions.**  control `ldq` (survives), then S1, then\n   the armed `ldq` (dies) -- **only `ioc_conf` changes between the two loads.**  Separate sessions\n   are still adequate, but this removes the residual that two processes could differ in something\n   that ran before the debugger prompt.\n4. **KEEP IT ON `step`.**  `continue` would make the `lca_interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` stubs a\n   confounder -- and `cpu_dyntrans.c` skips interrupt delivery while single-stepping anyway.\n\n*** THE CONTRACT LIMIT, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT LINE FOR THE FIX ROUND: THIS IS A WITNESS OF\nTHE `exit(1)`, NOT A DETECTOR OF THE REPAIR. ***  grok: *\"'Host survives, session completes' greens\nfor a silent `return 1` that skips `bus_pci_*` and `memory_writemax64`, for a latched announce, AND\nfor falling through into existing `bus_pci` with `cur_bus=1` (empty type-1 also survives).\"*  So the\npost-fix oracle cannot discriminate between fix members, and a round that ships one must build a row\nthat can.  **Post-fix caution in the same breath: if someone later implements type-1 by falling\nthrough to `bus_pci_setaddr(bus=1, ...)`, empty bus 1 is a non-killing miss today, so the witness\ngoes green -- do NOT read that green as \"type-1 is modelled.\"**\n\n**WHY OFFSET 0 IS THE RIGHT CONTROL ADDRESS, which I had asserted without warrant:** it is not\nHAE-like (HAE is an exact-address `break` in `lca_ioc`; `lca_pci_conf` has no per-offset special\ncase).  On the ARMED session the offset is irrelevant -- `bus == 1` exits before `tag`/`dev` are\nused.  On the SURVIVING control, `tag == 0` matches no IDSEL bit, so the `dev >= 21` body runs --\n**and that body is already the commented-out kill with `dev = 0;`** -- then an empty-slot read\nreturns `(uint64_t)-1` without exiting.  `0x18` (the NetBSD `| 0x3 << 3` encoding) yields the same\n`tag`/`reg` and is equivalent.  **Do NOT use `relative_addr = 0x800000`**, which talks to the\nregistered `i82378zb` at device 7.\n\n*** AND grok FOUND THE DESIGN ARGUMENT SITTING FIVE LINES ABOVE THE DEFECT: the `dev >= 21` arm is\nALREADY DE-FATALISED, in the same function, and the surviving control USES that path. ***  *\"If that\n`exit(1)` were still live, the control would die and you would misread PCI_CONF as unconditionally\nfatal.  The file already contains the decline precedent the `else` lacks.\"*  That is the strongest\navailable warrant for declining here, and it is one function away from the kill.\n\nAlso confirmed: FAULT (member 5) is unavailable -- `memory_rw.c` delivers a failed device access as\nan exception only under `MEM_MIPS`/`MEM_M88K`, and this path always `return 1` on the live path, so\nthe kill is `exit(1)` and not `return 0`.  And `lca_isa`'s `ofs > len` kill is a DIFFERENT window\n(`LCA_PCI_SIO`), not reachable from `0x1e0000000`.\n\n*** CROSS-REFERENCE, AND A CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN NOVELTY CLAIM.  [[lcabadval]] ALREADY\nENUMERATED THIS KILL ON 2026-09-06. ***\n\nIts eleven-kill census for dev_lca.c lists **line 134, class BUS, condition `bus != 0`, i.e.\n`d->ioc_conf & 1`** -- so the site AND its dependence on ioc_conf were both already recorded, and I\nfiled this row saying only lcabadval mentioned lca_pci_conf without reading how.  **Sixth\nrediscovery this session; see [[refilingclass]], which predicts exactly this.**\n\n**WHAT THIS ROW GENUINELY ADDS, stated narrowly:** the census records the MECHANISM; it does not\nrecord that **`0x1` PASSES the CONF guard**.  That is what makes the kill reachable with no rejected\nvalue anywhere on the path -- an accepted write in one handler arming a terminating branch in\nanother -- and it is why the disposition differs from lcabadval's six value guards, whose kills all\nfire on refusal.  Plus the witness design, now reviewed SOUND by grok.\n\n**KEPT AS A SEPARATE ROW** because it has its own disposition (decline the config access, or\nimplement bus != 0) and its own witness, and because folding it into a row named for VALUE GUARDS\nwould hide it -- which is the census's own closing lesson: *a row named after one class hides the\nmembers of the others.*\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS NAMES SOMETHING PLAUSIBLY WORSE THAN THIS ROW, WHICH I SHOULD SAY OUT LOUD RATHER\nTHAN LEAVE IN A TABLE: the two INTERRUPT STUBS at :73 and :85. ***  `lca_interrupt_assert` and\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` are bare `fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1)` with **NO GUARD AT ALL**, so *any*\ndevice asserting an interrupt through the LCA ends the process regardless of what it writes.  In the\ncensus's words: the value-guard registers *\"need a firmware revision that writes something else;\nthese two need only that an interrupt is asserted.\"*  That is member 7 (IMPLEMENT) of the option\nset -- a whole path stubbed out, with no value being refused -- and it is a stronger candidate for\nthe next critical round than this one.\n\n*** THE RUNG-3 WITNESS EXISTS AND THE DEFECT IS MEASURED, 2026-09-07.  regress/lca_confbus_probe.py,\nartifact regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw, sentinel LCACONFBUS_DONE. ***\n\n    bus ctl      yes                    yes                    CONTROL: identical read at ioc_conf==0 survived   ok\n    bus ctlsig   0xffffffffffffffff     0xffffffffffffffff     DEVICE SIGNATURE: empty-slot ID probe             ok\n    bus kill     dead                   alive                  <- THE DEFECT                                     FAIL\n    bus attrib   yes                    yes                    death carried THIS arm@s diagnostic               ok\n      EVIDENCE: TODO. Non-zero bus.\n    LCACONFBUS_RESULT=3/4\n\n**One emulator process, unmodified `alphabook1`, real guest instructions through real address\ndecode.**  The sequence is: guest `ldq` from `LCA_PCI_CONF+0` with `ioc_conf == 0` (SURVIVES), then\nguest `stq 0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` (ACCEPTED -- `idata & ~1` is 0), then the IDENTICAL `ldq` again\n(HOST DIES).  Only `ioc_conf` differs between the two loads, which is what attributes the death to\nthe VALUE rather than to the address, the width, or touching the window at all.\n\n*** grok@s PREDICTED DEVICE SIGNATURE CAME BACK EXACTLY. ***  It reasoned from `bus_pci.c` that an\nempty-slot read returns `0xffffffffffffffff` via `memory_writemax64` while UNMAPPED returns 0.  The\ncontrol row reads **0xffffffffffffffff**.  That is a prediction from source, confirmed by\nmeasurement -- so the signature control is established, not assumed.\n\n**ORACLE POLARITY is the ladder@s: the rows assert the CORRECT (post-fix) behaviour, so `bus kill`\nis RED on the committed build and goes GREEN when the defect is fixed, with the SAME script.**\nClause (i) is therefore HALF discharged -- the pre-fix symptom is shown; the post-fix green awaits a\nfix, which is correct for test-first.\n\n*** TWO APPARATUS DEFECTS CAUGHT IN THE FIRST RUN AND FIXED, both worth keeping. ***\n1. **The first artifact carried NO EVIDENCE for `bus attrib`.**  The row tested the PTY buffer and\n   printed nothing, so the preserved log showed a green row whose diagnostic a later reader could\n   not check.  The probe now ECHOES the matched line into its own output.  *A green row whose\n   evidence is not in the artifact is not verifiable, however sound the logic.*\n2. **I nearly filed a false correction to grok.**  It said the `TODO. Non-zero bus.` literal is\n   unique in `src/`; my grep returned TWO.  The second hit is `dev_lca.o`, the compiled object.  One\n   source site, `dev_lca.c:133`.  grok was right; my apparatus swept binaries.\n\n*** THE FIX DESIGN IS CLEARER THAN THIS ROW ASSUMED, AND IT DIVERGES FROM ITS SIBLING FOR A REASON\nWORTH STATING.  Derived 2026-09-07 from the codex fix design on [[lcairqstubkill]] plus this row's\nown witness. ***\n\n**THE TWO LCA ROWS LOOK LIKE ONE PROBLEM AND ARE NOT.**  Both are bare `fatal()+exit(1)` reached by\nordinary guest access, so it is tempting to give them one disposition.  The difference is what the\nguest is ASKING FOR:\n\n* [[lcairqstubkill]] -- an interrupt DELIVERY.  There is no correct answer that is not delivery: a\n  decline LOSES AN EVENT, silently or otherwise, and the guest can never learn of it.  codex's\n  disposition there is \"diagnose and stop the guest CPU\", because continuing is a lie.\n* **THIS ROW -- a PCI configuration cycle on bus 1.  There IS a correct answer, and hardware gives\n  it: a config read of an absent device returns ALL ONES.**  An unimplemented BUS is legitimately\n  EMPTY.  Declining is not a lost event; it is the architecturally right reply.\n\n> **So the dispositions properly differ, and the reason is not \"one is worse\".  For a DELIVERY path\n> an unimplemented target has no honest continuation; for an ADDRESSING path it has one, and the\n> tree already implements it.**\n\n*** AND THE TREE ALREADY DOES THE RIGHT THING ONE STEP DOWN, which the witness measured by accident.\n***  The `bus == 0` branch already reaches `bus_pci_setaddr` / `bus_pci_data_access`, and this row's\nown witness recorded the result: **a read of an empty slot returns `0xffffffffffffffff`** (the\n`bus ctlsig` row, MEASURED).  grok noted the same about the armed side in its design review --\n*\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*.\n\n**SO THE LIKELY FIX IS NOT A DECLINE AT ALL BUT A FALL-THROUGH:** stop special-casing `bus != 0`,\nlet the existing `bus_pci` path handle it, and an empty bus 1 answers all-ones by itself.  That is\noption-set member 7 (IMPLEMENT) reached cheaply, because the machinery exists -- unlike the sibling\nrow, where member 7 is blocked at the dyntrans core.\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS THEREFORE THE RETURNED VALUE, NOT THE RUNNING STATE. ***  codex's\noracle for the sibling row (\"stopped guest state, plus the reason\") is right THERE and wrong HERE,\nbecause a correct fix must leave the guest RUNNING.  The observable that separates the candidates\nhere is what the armed read returns:\n\n    fall through to bus_pci (empty bus 1)      0xffffffffffffffff    <- architecturally correct\n    silent return without servicing            0x0 or unchanged      <- looks like UNMAPPED\n    latched announce then return unserviced    0x0 or unchanged      <- same, plus a message\n    the existing exit(1)                       host gone\n\n**And this probe ALREADY HAS THAT ROW SHAPE**: `bus ctlsig` asserts exactly `0xffffffffffffffff` on\nthe bus-0 control, and distinguishes it from the `0x0` an unmapped read gives.  The fix round adds\nthe mirror of it on the ARMED side.  *No new capability is needed, which is not true of the sibling.*\n\n**NOT YET VERIFIED, and it is the one thing to check before building:** that `bus_pci` genuinely\ntolerates `cur_bus == 1` rather than asserting or indexing something sized for one bus.  grok's\nreview says empty type-1 survives; **that is a seat's reading and this row has not measured it.**\nThe cheap check is to build the fall-through and run the existing probe -- the armed row should turn\nfrom `dead` to `0xffffffffffffffff`.\n\n*** THE UNVERIFIED LINK IS CLOSED BY READING: `bus_pci' IS BUS-AGNOSTIC BY CONSTRUCTION, SO THE\nFALL-THROUGH FIX IS SOUND WITHOUT A NEW CAPABILITY. ***\n\nThe paragraph above flagged one thing to check before building -- whether `bus_pci' tolerates\n`cur_bus == 1' rather than asserting or indexing something sized for a single bus.  It does:\n\n    bus_pci.h:84   int  cur_bus, cur_device, cur_func, cur_reg;   <- a plain int, not bus-0-only\n    bus_pci.h:87   struct pci_device *first_device;               <- a LINKED LIST, not an array\n                                                                     indexed by bus number\n    bus_pci.c:102  if (dev->bus == pci_data->cur_bus && ...)      <- devices carry their OWN bus\n                                                                     field and are MATCHED against it\n    bus_pci.c:200  pci_data->cur_bus = bus;                       <- set from the caller, unchecked\n\n**So a lookup with `cur_bus = 1` walks the list, matches nothing (no device carries `bus == 1`), and\nfalls into the no-device path -- which this row's own witness has already MEASURED returning\n`0xffffffffffffffff` on bus 0.**  Nothing is sized for one bus and nothing asserts.\n\n*** THAT CONFIRMS grok's READING AND UPGRADES IT FROM A SEAT CLAIM TO A CHECKED ONE. ***  Its design\nreview said *\"falling through into existing bus_pci with cur_bus=1 (empty type-1 also survives)\"*,\nwhich this row correctly recorded as unmeasured.  It is now verified by source rather than accepted.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE: the fix is a DELETION, not an addition.**  Remove the `bus != 0` special case and let\nthe existing path serve the cycle; an absent device on an absent bus answers all-ones by itself.\nThat is the cheapest member-7 in the option set -- and the addition licence is satisfied trivially,\nbecause nothing is added.\n\n**WHAT STILL OWES A MEASUREMENT, and it is one run:** build the fall-through and re-run\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`.  **PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION: the `bus kill' row turns from `dead'\nto `alive', and a mirror of `bus ctlsig' on the armed side should read `0xffffffffffffffff`** -- the\nsame value the bus-0 control already returns, which is what distinguishes a serviced empty-bus cycle\nfrom a silent unserviced return (`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).\n\n*** PASS-1 FIX PANEL, 2026-09-07 -- PROVISIONAL: recorded on THREE landed seats (deepseek, minimax,\nthe kimi-k3:cloud substitute) while codex, grok and glm were still answering.  Any ruling short of\nthe roster is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AND MY WORRY WAS UNFOUNDED -- AND THE PROOF IS INSIDE THE EXISTING CODE, WHICH I HAD\nNOT THOUGHT TO USE.**  I asked whether the file's tag comment describes the same `tag` this code\ncomputes (`(relative_addr >> 5) & ~0xff`) or a pre-shift value.  minimax:\n\n> *\"The existing IDSEL check proves the code's `tag` IS the comment's `tag`.  The `bus == 0` branch\n> checks `tag & (0x800U << dev)` for dev 0..20.  The mask `1 << (dev+11)` only matches real hardware\n> if `tag` holds IDSEL bits at positions 11..31 -- exactly the comment's type-0 layout.  That branch\n> is EXERCISED TODAY on real bus-0 traffic and works, so the interpretation is FORCED.\"*\n\n`0x800` is exactly `1 << 11`, and the kimi substitute reaches it independently (*\"func at bits 10..8\nin both rows\"*).  **So `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the faithful type-1 decode.**  *A working branch\nof the same function is a stronger oracle for a layout question than the comment is -- the comment\ncould be stale, the working code cannot be.*\n\n*** Q2 IS THE REAL DEFECT IN MY SKETCH, AND ALL THREE SEATS FLAG IT: THE FIX AS WRITTEN WOULD BE\nINCOHERENT. ***  deepseek: *\"the sketch is incomplete/incoherent because it keeps\n`bus = d->ioc_conf & 1` while the type-1 tag comment says bus is in tag bits 23..16.\"*  The kimi\nsubstitute sharpens it: **the model computes THREE bus/type sources from the address -- the AD[1:0]\ntype bits, the tag's bus field, and `ioc_conf & 1` -- and DISCARDS TWO.**  *\"The packet cannot say\nwhich one real hardware honours.\"*\n\n> **So decoding `dev` from the type-1 layout while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two\n> addressing models.**  Either the tag is authoritative (and `bus` must come from bits 23..16) or the\n> register is (and the type-1 tag layout does not apply).  **The fix cannot be written until that is\n> chosen, and no seat could choose it from the packet.**\n\n**Q4: THE DISCRIMINATING ROW IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT -- unanimous among the three.**  My\nproposed armed-side all-ones row separates *serviced an empty bus* from *returned without servicing*\n(`0x0`, indistinguishable from unmapped).  **It cannot separate IMPLEMENT from an explicit all-ones\nreturn**, because an EMPTY bus 1 answers all-ones under both.  kimi: *\"only a populated bus-1 device\ncan\"* tell them apart -- and nothing in this tree places one.\n\n*That is a real limit on what this round can claim, and it is better to know it before building the\nrow than after.*\n\n**NOT ADOPTED PENDING THE ROSTER.**  The Q2 question is a hardware-behaviour question the packet\ncannot settle, and `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA/21066 datasheet (the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` list has\nDC21285 but no 21066), so it may not be settleable from this machine at all -- which would make it a\ncandidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for a fix round.\n\n*** grok LANDED AND SETTLED Q2 -- THE ANSWER THE PACKET-FED SEATS COULD NOT REACH, AND IT DISSOLVES\nTHE INCOHERENCE RATHER THAN CHOOSING A SIDE. ***\n\n> **VERDICT: IMPLEMENT, but the sketch is one assignment short.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is the\n> right extract of *this* `tag` (Q1 is not a double-shift).  **Also assign\n> `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` -- `ioc_conf & 1` is the PCI cycle TYPE, not the bus number.**\n> Member 7; fall through into `bus_pci`.  The proposed all-ones row is necessary and not sufficient:\n> the witness address is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract.\n\n**WHY THIS RESOLVES THE CONFLICT INSTEAD OF PICKING A WINNER.**  deepseek and the kimi substitute\nwere right that taking `dev` from the tag while taking `bus` from a register bit mixes two addressing\nmodels.  grok's reading shows the register bit is not a bus selector at all: it chooses the CYCLE\nTYPE -- type 0 addresses the local bus, type 1 addresses a bus behind a bridge -- and in type 1 the\nbus number comes from the tag.  **The file's own comment, \"the PCI bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\",\nis a loose description of exactly that**, so the code comment and grok agree once the names are\nstraightened.  *The variable is simply misnamed: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;` stores a cycle type in\nsomething called `bus`.*\n\n**THE FIX IS THEREFORE TWO ASSIGNMENTS, NOT ONE:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16  */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11  */\n    }\n\n*** AND grok BREAKS MY PROPOSED DISCRIMINATING ROW FOR A REASON I HAD NOT SEEN: \"the witness address\nis not a type-1 tag\". ***  The existing probe reads at `LCA_PCI_CONF + 0`, so `tag == 0` -- which\nunder the type-1 decode means bus 0, device 0.  **The row would therefore exercise the new code with\na degenerate tag and prove nothing about the extract**, and \"empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"\nbecause an absent device answers all-ones whatever bus/dev were decoded.\n\n> **So the round needs a row that drives a NON-ZERO type-1 tag and observes something that depends\n> on the decoded bus/dev -- and with no device on bus 1, there is nothing to observe.**  That is the\n> same limit the three packet-fed seats reached from the other side (*\"only a populated bus-1 device\n> can\"* separate IMPLEMENT from a faked all-ones return).  Two independent routes to one conclusion.\n\n**HARDWARE CLAIM, FLAGGED AS UNVERIFIABLE FROM THIS MACHINE:** that `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type\nis a statement about the 21066/LCA, and **`_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet** -- the PDF list has\nDC21285 but nothing for this chipset.  It is consistent with the file's comment and with standard PCI\ntype-0/type-1 addressing, but it is not CHECKED here, and this project's rule is to cite only what\nhas been read.  **That is a good candidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for me.**\n\n*** THE PANEL IS COMPLETE (6 seats) AND UNANIMOUS ON THE DESIGN -- AND codex CITED A PRIMARY SOURCE\nSITTING ON THIS MACHINE THAT I HAD JUST DECLARED UNAVAILABLE. ***\n\nI wrote, one paragraph above, that the cycle-type claim is *\"a statement about the 21066 ... not\nCHECKED here\"* because `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet.  **codex pointed at\n`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` -- the REAL OpenBSD LCA driver, 4,925 bytes,\ndated 1997, on disk in this project.**  Verified and read:\n\n    :103   if (bp != NULL)  *bp = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;      /*  BUS      */\n    :106   if (dp != NULL)  *dp = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;      /*  DEVICE   */\n    :108   if (fp != NULL)  *fp = (tag >>  8) & 0x7;       /*  FUNCTION */\n\n**That is grok's proposal, verbatim, from the driver the hardware was written for.**  `bus` is bits\n23..16 of the tag and `dev` is bits 15..11 -- not inference, not a datasheet paraphrase, the actual\ndecomposition routine.\n\n*** AND `lca_conf_read` SETTLES Q2 OUTRIGHT: ***\n\n    :125   /* secondary if bus # != 0 */\n    :126   pci_decompose_tag(&lcp->lc_pc, tag, &secondary, &device, 0);\n    :127   if (secondary) {\n    :130           REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01;\n    :132   } else {\n    :134           /*  on the LCA, must frob the tag used for devices on the primary bus,\n    :136              in the same ways as is used by type 1 configuration cycles on PCs.  */\n    :139           tag = (1 << (device + 11)) | (tag & 0x7ff);\n    :140   }\n\n**The driver derives the bus from the TAG and then writes `IOC_CONF = 0x01` to select the SECONDARY\nconfiguration FORMAT.**  So `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type, exactly as grok and codex said and as\nthe fork's own loosely-worded comment (\"bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit\") was groping at.  **The\nfork's variable is misnamed**, and its type-0 handling is correct -- `(1 << (device + 11))` is\nprecisely the IDSEL form the `0x800U << dev` loop scans for.\n\n*** THE ROW IS MASSIVELY STRENGTHENED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***  My witness writes\n`0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` and then performs a config access, and I described that as an ordinary but\nsynthetic pair of guest accesses.  **It is not synthetic: it is the OpenBSD driver's own\nsecondary-bus path, line for line.**  `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` followed by the config read is\nwhat `lca_conf_read` DOES whenever it is asked about a device behind a bridge.\n\n> **So the kill is not merely reachable in principle -- it is on the code path a real guest driver\n> takes the moment it probes a secondary bus.**  The row previously said \"NOT CLAIMED: that any\n> shipped guest issues this sequence.\"  That caution can now be withdrawn: a shipped guest driver\n> issues exactly this sequence, by construction, for secondary-bus config cycles.\n\n**THE FIX, now backed by the driver rather than by reading the fork's comment:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16   */\n            dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  type-1: device is bits 15..11 */\n    }\n\n**WHAT IS STILL NOT SETTLED, and every seat agrees:** the discriminating row.  grok -- *\"the witness\naddress is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract\"*; glm -- the row *\"conflates\nIMPLEMENT with explicit-all-ones DECLINE, silent with latched, and can false-pass on stale all-ones\nunless the destination register is pre-seeded\"*; the kimi substitute and minimax -- only a POPULATED\nbus-1 device could separate them.  **Four independent routes to one wall.**  glm's pre-seeding point\nis the one actionable improvement: seed the destination register so a stale all-ones cannot pass.\n\n*The lesson about this machine is the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` lesson again, one directory over: I\ndeclared a fact unverifiable while its primary source sat in the project.  **Before calling a\nhardware question unanswerable here, look for the guest OS source tree.***\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** codex: REVISE.  THE SHIFT IS RIGHT AND ALMOST EVERYTHING AROUND IT NEEDED CORRECTING --\nINCLUDING TWO PREMISES I STATED AS SETTLED. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, with a denominator.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is correct: `relative_addr >> 5`\nundoes the address expansion, so for a valid tag T and aligned register R,\n`A = (T << 5) | (R << 5) | 24` gives `((A >> 5) & ~0xff) == T`, putting device bits at 15..11 of\nthe recovered tag.  Validated over **4,194,304 encode/decode combinations, zero mismatches** --\nand codex labelled that an ARITHMETIC check, not an emulator test, which is the honest form.\n\n*** MY IMPLEMENT SKETCH WAS INCOMPLETE: IT DECODED `dev` AND NOT `bus`. ***  `IOC_CONF` selects\nthe CYCLE FORMAT, it does not supply the bus number -- PCI address bits 23..2 come from CPU\naddress bits 28..7, and only bits 1..0 come from `IOC_CONF[1:0]`.  So the minimum correction is\n\n    } else {\n        bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;\n        dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;\n    }\n\n> **AND THE TRAP IN THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE, which I would have walked into: DO NOT CHOOSE THE\n> DECODING BRANCH FROM THE EXTRACTED BUS FIELD.**  Primary-bus IDSEL bits overlap that field, and\n> keeping `bus == 1` in the forwarding call **collapses every type-1 destination onto bus 1**.\n\n*** PREMISE 4 IS FALSE AS STATED, and the refutation is one function away. ***  I wrote that an\nempty-slot read returns all-ones.  `bus_pci_data_access` implements a THREE-WAY contract:\n\n    read, register 0        ->  0xffffffffffffffff\n    read, any other reg     ->  ZERO\n    write                   ->  a diagnostic on EVERY access, then returns\n\nThe measured ID-register instance stands; the GENERAL claim does not.  **This matters because it\nmeans the candidate dispositions differ TODAY, with no bus-1 device added at all** -- an\nunconditional all-ones responder differs from the backend at nonzero registers, and announcement\npolicies differ on diagnostic output.  (The write diagnostic does not terminate: `fatal()` returns\nnormally.)\n\n*** THE DISCRIMINATING ROW I PROPOSED CANNOT DISCRIMINATE, AND IT HAS THREE FALSE-POSITIVE TRAPS.\n***  At register 0, all-ones is returned by correct forwarding, by the INCORRECT bus-fixed-to-1\nforwarding, by explicit all-ones completion, and by announced all-ones completion -- four\ndispositions, one observable.  Worse:\n\n* **THE EXISTING ADDRESS DOES NOT ENCODE BUS 1.**  `lca_confbus_probe.py`'s setup uses a ZERO\n  window offset, so the recovered tag -- bus field included -- is zero, and the control side\n  reaches the **no-IDSEL fallback to device 0**.  *The witness establishes mode-dependent\n  termination, not a correctly addressed secondary-bus transaction.*  That is a scope correction\n  to what this row's existing witness is entitled to claim.\n* **A FAILED LOAD PRESERVES THE CONTROL SIGNATURE.**  `memory_rw` pre-zeros, so a handler that\n  returns success without filling data yields zero -- but a literal `return 0` takes the FAILURE\n  path, and `LS_GENERIC_N` then returns *before updating the destination register*, so the witness\n  **reads back the all-ones the register already held.**  A vacuity trap of exactly the shape the\n  carrier's witness clauses exist to catch.\n* **THE PARSER TAKES THE FIRST OBSERVATION** (`printed` uses `re.search`), so adding another print\n  and calling the helper again retrieves the CONTROL's result rather than the new one.\n\n**WHAT IT PRESCRIBES INSTEAD:** keep the signature check as an empty-ID COMPLETION check with an\nindependently identified result and a distinct prior destination value, and add a **model-level\nrouting fixture** observing the exact bus/device/function/register passed onward -- distinct\nsynthetic devices on bus 1 and on another bus separate correct decoding from hardcoded bus 1 and\nfrom constant-return stubs.  Include write dispatch and preservation of primary-bus decoding.\n\n*** A SECOND ARCHITECTURAL PREMISE CORRECTED: RAW LCA ABSENCE DOES NOT GUARANTEE ALL-ONES. ***\nDigital's §6.2.18 says an unanswered transaction is a MASTER ABORT: the read returns *arbitrary\ndata with error status*, raising an instruction exception plus interrupt and IOC error logging.\n**So the 64-bit all-ones signature is an EMULATOR-COMPATIBILITY oracle, not a hardware contract**,\nand the round may not claim hardware fidelity for it.  The current path delegates to the generic\nbackend and returns success; it implements none of that error sequence.\n\n**SCOPE, stated so the round does not overclaim what it closes:** this closes the configuration\nhandler's termination and its missing destination decode ONLY.  Still open in the same file --\n`lca_ioc`'s unconditional read/write `default` termination and its register-value rejections;\n`lca_interrupt_assert` / `lca_interrupt_deassert`; and `lca_isa`'s separate guard.  Nor does\nforwarding implement bridge topology or configuration-cycle error reporting: the backend matches a\nnumeric tuple and receives **no cycle-type argument at all**, so type 1 with a zero bus field needs\nan explicit policy or simple forwarding will alias a root-bus device.\n\n**PRIMARY SOURCE, and this project did not have it:** codex cited the *Digital 21066/21066A\nHardware Reference Manual* (Table 6-1, §6.4.2, §6.2.18) with page anchors.  No 21066/LCA manual was\nin `_scratchpad/`, and there are several open LCA rows reasoning from source alone.  Fetched at the\ncitation, on the `vmesbcp2.pdf` precedent.\n\n*** AND THEN VERIFIED LOCALLY RATHER THAN TRUSTED, which is what the carrier's rule demands and\nwhich this project has been burned by skipping: `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipped DDI 0100I page numbers\nthat came from panel output rather than a manual. ***  `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt`, 713 KB /\n13,876 lines:\n\n    :8840  S6.2.6   \"CPU physical address bits <31:7> are used for PCI address bits <26:2>\"\n                    -> THE SHIFT OF 5 that `relative_addr >> 5` implements.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9216  S6.2.9   \"PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type\n                     register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0>\"\n                    -> IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and does NOT supply the bus.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9486  S6.2.18  \"the IOC returns arbitrary data with error status to the CPU, which causes\n                     an instruction exception\" + internal interrupt + ERR/CODE=1/CMD/ADDR/LOST\n                    -> absent-device reads are NOT all-ones on real hardware.  CONFIRMED.\n\n**One narrowing, stated because a wrong reason gets reused:** codex wrote *\"bits 23..2 from CPU\naddress bits 28..7\"*.  The manual's general sparse rule is the WIDER `<31:7> -> <26:2>` with the\nsame shift; codex's range is the PCI **type-1 config format** (bus 23..16, dev 15..11, fn 10..8,\nreg 7..2), which S6.2.9 defers to explicitly -- *\"restricted by the encoding requirements of PCI\nconfiguration cycles as described in the PCI Local Bus [Specification]\"*.  **Same shift, narrower\nwindow, and the narrowing is correct for config cycles** -- so `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff` and\n`dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` rest on the PCI spec's field layout, not on this manual, and the round\nshould say so rather than citing the LCA manual for the field positions.\n\n*** A TRAP FOR THE NEXT READER: `file` REPORTS THIS PDF AS \"10 page(s)\" AND IT IS WRONG. ***  The\nreal count lives in a linearized trailer.  I nearly discarded the download as truncated because\ncodex had cited `#page=194` and `#page=211`; `pdftotext -layout` settled it in one command.  *A\nsize or page check that disagrees with a citation is a reason to extract, not to discard.*\n\n*** I RE-DERIVED THIS ROW'S GUEST-SOURCE EVIDENCE FROM SCRATCH AND EVERY ELEMENT OF IT WAS ALREADY\nHERE.  Caught before committing, by grepping this note instead of trusting my memory of it. ***\n\nI read `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` and wrote up four findings as new: that\n`lca_conf_read` writes `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` on the secondary-bus path; that `data` is\ninitialised to `(pcireg_t)-1` and overwritten only if `badaddr()` succeeds, so absence normalises to\nall-ones at ANY offset; that the primary-bus tag frob places an IDSEL bit at `1 << (device + 11)`;\nand the decomposition itself.  **Counted in this very note before adding them: `REGVAL` 4, `IDSEL`\n7, `device + 11` 4, `badaddr` 2, `pcireg_t)-1` 1.**  All four were already recorded, several in the\nsame words.\n\n**THE CAUSE IS EXACT AND IT IS THE SECOND TIME TODAY: I READ PART OF A RECORD AND ACTED.**  I\nqueried this row's `state_note` and looked at the first fifteen hundred characters, which covered\nthe defect and the shipped witness but stopped short of the guest-source section.  Earlier the same\nday I read a row's prose and never read its `witness` FIELD, and built a second witness for a row\nthat already had one.  *Different field, same failure: a partial read of a record that already\ncontained the answer.*\n\n**WHAT IS ACTUALLY NEW FROM MY PASS is one caveat, and it belongs to the reachability claim rather\nthan to the decode:** the driver's `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` sits behind `if (secondary)`, so it\nexecutes only when something addresses a bus other than zero -- and **whether `alphabook1` ever\npresents a secondary bus is not established anywhere in this row.**  `devinit_lca` adds only\n`i82378zb` at 0:7:0.  So *the driver contains the sequence unconditionally; whether a booting guest\nexecutes it is a topology fact nobody here has measured.*  That bounds how far the guest source can\ncarry the reachability argument, and it is the one line worth keeping from an otherwise redundant\nre-derivation.\n\n*The `guestsrcunknown` row deliberately FILED its proposed carrier line for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication rather than editing the carrier unilaterally.  I have honoured that: today's two\ncarrier edits were INVENTORY entries in an existing PDF list, not a new behavioural rule.*\n\n*** THE REVISED DESIGN IS A DIFF, NOT A SHAPE -- AND IT REVERSES ITS OWN EARLIER SCOPE POSITION. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Include the bus-zero alias in this round.  A single-handler diff can fix it without\nchanging the backend API.  My earlier proposal was incomplete; the replacement observer remains\nunvalidated here.\"*\n\n**THE KEY CORRECTION IS ONE VARIABLE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE DEFECT IN MINIATURE:** the handler used a\nsingle `bus` for two different things -- the CYCLE TYPE selected by `ioc_conf & 1`, and the\nDESTINATION bus.  The diff splits them:\n\n    -\tint tag, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    +\tint tag, type1, bus, dev, func, reg;\n    ...\n    -\tbus = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    -\tif (bus == 0) {\n    +\ttype1 = d->ioc_conf & 1;\n    +\tbus = 0;\n    +\tif (!type1) {\n\nthen decodes the real destination on the type-1 arm -- `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;` -- replacing the `fatal(); exit(1)`.\n\n*** AND IT CLOSES THE ALIAS IT ITSELF FOUND, WHICH THE EARLIER \"MINIMUM CORRECTION\" DID NOT: ***\n\n    +\tif (type1 && bus == 0) {\n    +\t\t/*  Type-1 cycles do not select primary-bus devices.\n    +\t\t    Match bus_pci's empty-slot reads; discard writes.  */\n    +\t\todata = reg == 0 ? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;\n    +\t} else {\n    \t\tbus_pci_setaddr(...); bus_pci_data_access(...);\n    +\t}\n\n**The refusal covers reads AND writes and leaves backend state untouched**, and its return values\nmatch `bus_pci_data_access`'s own empty-slot contract -- all-ones at register 0, zero elsewhere --\nwhich is the contract I stated WRONGLY as a premise two passes ago and which codex corrected.\n\n> **SO THE ROUND'S SCOPE QUESTION IS ANSWERED AGAINST MY NARROWER FRAMING.**  I asked it to argue\n> the narrower round first; it did, and then held that the alias must land in the same diff -- on\n> the ground that forwarding a type-1 cycle with bus zero would select the existing primary-bus\n> `i82378zb`, so a round that decodes `bus` and stops there ships a NEW misrouting in place of a\n> termination.\n\n**HONEST LIMITS, stated by the seat rather than extracted from it:** the diff is **unapplied and\nuncompiled** -- it passes `git apply --check` and nothing more, because that session is read-only --\nand **the replacement observer remains unvalidated**, so the round still has no measurement lane\nbeyond the existing 3/4 witness.  *A design that says which of its parts are untested is worth more\nthan one that does not, and this round must not present the diff as measured.*\n\n*** #515 IMPLEMENTED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-08.  6/10 PRE-FIX -> 10/10 POST-FIX, and the round found\na FLOOR BREACH IN ITS OWN DETECTOR before any table ran. ***\n\n**THE DIFF** is codex's revised design applied unchanged in substance: `bus' split into `type1'\n(the CYCLE TYPE, `d->ioc_conf & 1') and `bus' (the DESTINATION, decoded from the address), the\n`fatal(\"TODO. Non-zero bus.\"); exit(1)' arm replaced by `bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff; dev = (tag >> 11)\n& 0x1f;', and a refusal arm for the type-1/bus-zero alias returning `bus_pci_data_access''s own\nempty-slot values.  40 insertions, 9 deletions, 47 changed lines.\n\n**THE FUNCTION TAIL IS PRESERVED ON BOTH ARMS** -- `memory_writemax64` and `return 1` are still\nreached, so this is not the early-`return' sketch four readers once proposed into a different\nfunction.  Checked by reading the whole function after the edit, not by reading the diff.\n\n*** MEASUREMENT, in a scratch clone with the baseline NAMED and REBUILT. ***  `/tmp/gxlca`, a\nrelocatable `cp -a` of `build/`; baseline = git HEAD `5d3f82c`; `dev_lca.c` `cmp`-clean against SEC\nbefore mutant 1; baseline binary rebuilt IN THE CLONE with `[ binary -nt source ]` checked.\n`build/`, `est/`, `build-asan/` and the WSL tree were NOT touched.\n\n    PRE-FIX   LCACONFBUS_RESULT=6/10    pass2_20260908/lca_pre.raw\n    POST-FIX  LCACONFBUS_RESULT=10/10   pass2_20260908/lca_post.raw\n\nBoth verified by their completion sentinel (`LCACONFBUS_DONE`), not by existence.\n\n**THE ANCHOR ROW PRINTED THE DERIVED TUPLE EXACTLY**, which is the whole point of an anchored row:\n\n    EVIDENCE(S2): [ bus_pci_data_access(): write to non-existant device, bus 1 func 2 device 3 ]\n\nAddresses were derived from the handler's own two decode lines and then CONFIRMED by that line --\n`bus 1 dev 3 func 2` from `relative_addr = 0x234000`.  The three fields are deliberately DISTINCT\nand non-zero so a swapped field cannot pass by coincidence.\n\n*** TWO DEFECTS IN MY OWN DETECTOR, BOTH FOUND BEFORE COMMIT, AND THE SECOND IS A FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n**(1) A VACUOUS ROW, caught by the PRE-FIX RUN rather than by review.**  `t1 alias' first read\n`refused if ALIAS not in txt2' and scored GREEN on the pre-fix build -- where the host had already\ndied at the previous access and the alias write NEVER EXECUTED.  An absence-of-text oracle cannot\ndistinguish *\"ran and was refused\"* from *\"never ran\"*: the pattern-that-matched-nothing class, as a\nphantom absence read as a result.  Bound to `alive2' and now renders `died-before-E'.  *The row\nwould have shipped green-for-the-wrong-reason on the very build it exists to indict.*\n\n**(2) A CLAUSE-(d) NULL-REPAIR MUTANT THAT SWEEPS A 10-ROW DETECTOR CLEAN, found by walking the\nCHANNEL line at row-writing time.**  The refusal's repair is one expression -- `odata = reg == 0?\n(uint64_t) -1 : 0'.  Null it to `odata = 0' and every one of the ten rows still passes: the alias\nwas exercised only by a WRITE, whose `odata' is never read, and both control reads take the type-0\npath.  ROW `t1 rdval' added -- a type-1 READ at the alias, pinning the synthesised value.\n\n> *** MY OWN PASS-2 BRIEF ASSERTED THAT CELL WAS UNOBSERVABLE, AND IT IS NOT. ***  I wrote that\n> (type-1, bus == 0, read) *\"returns the same all-ones value as the control, so a read oracle cannot\n> see it\"*.  That conflates two different accesses: the control is a TYPE-0 empty-slot read served\n> by `bus_pci`, and this is the REFUSAL arm's own synthesised value.  They agree under the fix and\n> DISAGREE under the mutant, which is exactly what makes a row possible.  The brief had already gone\n> to the panel when I found it; it goes to the adjudication pass as my own correction.\n\n*** THE PRE-TABLE CHECK WAS RUN AND ITS OUTPUT RECORDED BEFORE TABLE 1, mtime 21:35:00 against a\nbrief of 21:32:10 -- the order is the proof, and it is the artifact rather than an assertion. ***\n`pass2_20260908/PRETABLE.out`, all four checks with `N/A` discharged WITH REASONS on the three\ncontrol rows rather than left blank.\n\n> **AND THE CARRIER ASKED THE FIRST MANDATED APPLICATION TO *CHECK* THE CHANNEL LINE, NOT MERELY\n> OBEY IT -- because a freshly minted rule's exemplar is a hypothesis.  THE ANSWER IS THAT ITS CLAIM\n> HELD.**  The claim is that a row's channel-under-its-mutant is knowable at WRITING time, before\n> any table runs.  It was, and doing it found finding (2) above -- a floor breach -- with no build\n> and no measurement.  Recorded as a measurement OF the rule, not as praise for it.\n\n*** THE ASK-AGAIN: ONE CAPPED QUERY, THREE REAL ESCAPES, ALL THE SAME FAMILY.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nTable 2 came back CLEAN -- ten variants, zero escapes -- so the round spent its single capped\nadversarial query.  **The cap counts ASKS, never FINDINGS**, so all three are disposed of here rather\nthan one taken and two filed.\n\n    deepseek   `if (type1 && bus == 0 && len == 8)'\n               Every row used an 8-byte access, so all fourteen stay GREEN, while a 1/2/4-byte\n               type-1 bus-0 access bypasses the refusal and reaches `bus_pci_data_access' --\n               including the REAL device at 0:7:0.\n    kimi       `func = (tag >> 8) & 7'  ->  `& 3'\n               The table's only func probe was the anchor's func 2 (binary 010), which never\n               exercises the field's TOP bit.  Functions 4-7 silently alias onto 0-3, so a config\n               cycle aimed at dev 7 FUNC 4 matches the registered func-0 device.\n    codex      the `reg != 0' branch of the refusal's own expression\n               *** RANKED ABOVE THE OTHER TWO BY THE SEAT ITSELF, and it is right. ***  `len' and\n               `func' are values no row varies; `reg != 0' is a WHOLE BRANCH of `odata = reg == 0?\n               (uint64_t) -1 : 0' that nothing reached.  Killing `M2_nullval' proves sensitivity to\n               the register-ZERO result and establishes nothing about the required zero elsewhere.\n\n> **ONE FAMILY: AN AXIS EVERY EXISTING ROW HELD CONSTANT.**  len was 8 everywhere, func's top bit was\n> never set, reg was 0 everywhere.  Fourteen rows, three frozen axes -- and the rows were mutually\n> reinforcing on the axes they DID vary, which is exactly what makes a frozen axis invisible.\n\n*** AND THE THIRD ONE WAS ALREADY WRITTEN DOWN IN THIS ROUND'S OWN PRE-TABLE BLOCK AND WALKED PAST. ***\nSection 2 of `PRETABLE.out` says, in its own words: *\"THE `zero elsewhere' HALF IS NOT ROWED ...\nDISCLOSED, not silently skipped ... Coverage debt, clause (e).\"*  A disclosure is an honest record and\nit is not a row; **the seat found in one query what the round had already identified and deferred.**\nThat is the unread-review shape aimed at a round's own artifact rather than at a seat's answer.\n\n**FOUR ROWS ADDED, 14 -> 18** (`t1 len4`, `t0 func4`, `t1 reg0`, `t1 reg4`).  All L1 construction --\ncaught before the detector shipped.\n\n**DISPOSITION, stated rather than assumed.**  Under the body-tested-partition reading, NONE of the\nthree is a floor breach: `lca_pci_conf` tests `writeflag' in its own code but never branches on\n`len' or `func' or `reg', so they induce no partition and fall to clause (e).  **They are closed\nanyway, and the warrant is the CLAIM -> ROW check rather than the floor:** each one falsifies a claim\nthe round's own comment makes -- *\"a type-1 cycle whose bus field is zero selects NOTHING\"* -- and a\nsentence asserting an observable consequence owes a row.\n\n**THE REGISTER PAIR IS ONE SESSION ON PURPOSE.**  A bare *\"reg 4 returns 0\"* row would be vacuous:\nUNMAPPED space also reads 0, which is precisely why the pre-table block deferred it.  Pairing it with\nthe reg-0 read in the SAME process makes the PAIR the oracle -- all-ones then zero, same window, only\nthe register changing -- so a dead window cannot produce it.\n\n*** A SEAT NOTE THAT MATTERS BEYOND THIS ROUND: codex REFUSED THE ASK-AGAIN IN ITS NORMAL FORM, AND\nTHE REFUSAL WAS PREDICTED. ***  The ask-again's whole purpose is to obtain *\"the exact source edit as\na diff hunk\"* that re-opens a closed defect -- construction in its purest form, which\n[[codexcyberflag]] says is filtered.  It was, at 6,339 bytes.  **Reframed to ask for the GAP instead\nof the EDIT** -- *\"name the input-space cell these rows leave least constrained; describe it as a\nproperty, not an edit\"* -- **the same seat answered, and produced the best of the three findings.**\nThe ask-again is structurally a construction task, so every future one needs that reframe.\n\n*** THE LAST TWO SEATS LANDED AND BOTH HIT THE SAME AXIS.  FOUR OF SIX SEATS CONVERGED ON `reg'\nINDEPENDENTLY -- and one of the two is a SHAPE MY OWN MUTANT DOES NOT COVER. ***\n\nThey were still streaming when the panel was first read (grok 230 b, minimax absent) and were NOT\nscored as failures; they finished at 3,600 and 4,549 bytes.  *Sampling once is not a death\ncertificate, and this is the second time today that rule has paid.*\n\n    minimax   `odata = reg == 0? (uint64_t) -1 : 0;`  ->  `odata = (uint64_t) -1;`\n              -- IDENTICAL to `M12_regnull`, which was already in table 3.  Independent arrival at\n              the same mutant is worth recording as corroboration, not as a new finding.\n    grok      `if (type1 && bus == 0)`  ->  `if (type1 && bus == 0 && reg == 0)`\n              *** A DIFFERENT AND STRICTLY WORSE SHAPE. ***\n\n**WHY grok's IS WORSE, and why `M12_regnull` does not cover it.**  `M12` nulls the ternary's\nelse-VALUE: a non-zero register still takes the refusal arm and merely gets the wrong answer.\n**grok's hoists `reg == 0` into the GUARD, so a non-zero register does not take the refusal arm at\nall -- it is FORWARDED, and at 0:7:0 it reaches the real `i82378zb`.**  That re-opens the exact\nmisrouting this round exists to prevent, at every register except 0.\n\n**AND IT COMES WITH THE MAINTAINER'S REASONING, which is what makes it a realistic edit rather than\nan adversarial one** -- grok's own words: *\"A maintainer looking at the ternary and at\n`bus_pci_data_access`'s `dev == NULL` arm will hoist `reg == 0` into the guard: 'the special case is\nonly the ID register; other registers already return 0 for a missing device.'  On an empty slot that\nis even true for reads.  On `i82378zb` it is not.\"*\n\n> **THAT IS THE dev-0 BLINDNESS AGAIN, ONE LEVEL UP.**  The maintainer's premise is TRUE at every\n> empty slot and FALSE at the one populated one -- which is precisely why every row this round added\n> late is aimed at 0:7:0.\n\n**PREDICTED to be caught by `t1 reg4`**: under grok's mutant that access is forwarded to 0:7:0\nregister 4, where the `i82378zb` holds `0x00000003`, against a want of `0x0`.  *** PREDICTED, NOT\nMEASURED -- and this project's rule is that a prediction in a measured column is the frozen-number\nclass.  A targeted two-build table (`base` + `M13_regconj`) is OWED before the round may claim the\nrow kills it. ***\n\n**SEAT TALLY ON THE `reg` AXIS: codex NAMED the gap as the least-constrained cell and ranked it\nabove `len` and `func`; grok and minimax each independently BUILT a mutant on it; and this round's\nown `PRETABLE.out` had DISCLOSED it as clause-(e) debt hours earlier and walked past it.**  Four\nindependent arrivals at one hole is not a coincidence -- it is what an axis every row holds constant\nlooks like from outside.\n\n*** grok PRESCRIBED THE ROW I HAD ALREADY BUILT, AND NAMED TWO RESIDUALS BEYOND IT.  Both are\nDISCLOSED under clause (e) rather than built, and the reasoning for stopping is grok's own. ***\n\nIts closing prescription: *\"If M10_regconj comes back 14/14, the row it wants is a type-1 access at\n0:7:0 **register 4**, both directions, with the same anti-wants as `t1 dev7rd` / `t1 dev7wr` (real\nconfig data / `device 7, function 0 (i82378zb)`).  **A value-0 row at an empty slot will not do.**\"*\n\n**`t1 reg4` IS at 0:7:0 register 4** -- `T1_D7R4 = LCA_PCI_CONF + 0x070080 + BYTE_ENABLES`, re-derived\nas tag 0x3800 -> bus 0, dev 7, func 0, reg 0x04.  So the address half of the prescription was already\nmet, and grok's warning about an empty slot is exactly why the pre-table block had deferred a bare\n`reg != 0` row in the first place.\n\n**RESIDUAL 1 -- ONE DIRECTION, NOT BOTH.**  I built the READ at reg 4 and not the WRITE.  The read\nalone kills `M13_regconj` (the forward returns the device's `0x00000003` against a want of `0x0`), so\nthe mutant in hand is covered; what is NOT covered is a variant that spares reads at non-zero\nregisters and forwards only writes.  **DISCLOSED, not built.**\n\n**RESIDUAL 2 -- THE BUS AXIS, and grok named it while explicitly declining to propose it:**\n\n> *\"Not proposing: `bus = 1` in the type-1 branch (collapses every non-zero bus onto the one\n> `t1 anchor` samples).  That also sweeps, and it is the original cycle-type/bus conflation without\n> the `exit(1)`.  It does not hit 0:7:0 -- those tags get bus 1, which is empty -- so it is a decode\n> collapse, not a reopening of the misrouting.  **A later bus-2 tuple row would catch it; this one\n> does not need that row to be a defect.**\"*\n\n**That is a FOURTH frozen axis** -- every type-1 row uses bus 0 or bus 1, so hardcoding `bus = 1`\nsweeps.  It is a real defect and a LESSER one: it collapses the decode without reaching the populated\nslot.  **A single bus-2 tuple row closes it.**\n\n*** WHY BOTH ARE FILED RATHER THAN BUILT, stated so it is a decision and not an omission. ***  The\ndetector has already grown 4 -> 18 rows inside one round, three of those additions from this single\nask.  Clause (e) exists precisely for this: **a contract that names what it does not cover is\nfalsifiable; one that claims completeness is not.**  grok's own framing supports stopping -- *\"this\none does not need that row to be a defect\"* -- and the probe header will carry both residuals by\nname, so a later escape is *\"outside the contract, as stated\"* rather than an argument about\noverclaiming.\n\n**The round therefore ships an 18-row detector with TWO NAMED HOLES: the write direction at a\nnon-zero register, and any bus value other than 0 or 1.**\n\n*** I PARKED A MAIN-LOOP NOTE IN `entries` AND THE DASHBOARD GENERATOR REFUSED TO RENDER.  It was\nright, and [[sgirandomcolour]] records this exact failure -- which I then repeated. ***\n\n`gen_dashboard.py` stopped with *\"'main-loop' 1 entries across 1 rows: lcaconfbus1kill.  Fix the\nledger (or add the column).  Declared: agy, codex, deepseek, fable5, glm, grok, kimi, minimax,\nopus5\"*.\n\n**`entries` is the SEAT ANSWER list, not a general log.**  `check_stage_panels.py` counts entries to\ndecide whether a stage's nine seats were fired -- so a main-loop note parked there is not merely\nmis-filed, it **MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER HAPPENED**, which is the\nseat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  The entry has been removed and its content lives\nin this note, where it belongs.\n\n*The lesson is not \"remember the convention\".  It is that the generator REFUSES rather than warns,\nso the mistake cannot ship -- and `sgirandomcolour` records seven such entries migrated for the same\nreason on an earlier day.  A rule written in a row's prose did not stop me; the tool did.*",
