-   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **NOT a new discovery of the SITE** -- [[lcabadval]]'s eleven-kill census already\nenumerated `:73` and `:85` and called them *\"plausibly the most severe of the eleven\"*.  What was\nmissing is a ROW: the census names them inside a row titled for VALUE GUARDS, which is the census's\nown closing lesson (*a row named after one class hides the members of the others*), and\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` mentioned them **zero** times.\n\n**THE SITE, verbatim -- there is no predicate at all:**\n\n    void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)\n    {\n            fatal(\"lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n    void lca_interrupt_deassert(struct interrupt *interrupt)\n    {\n            fatal(\"lca_interrupt_deassert: TODO\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n*** THE WIRING, DERIVED BY READING `DEVINIT(lca)` -- AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS SEVERE. ***\n\n    memset(&interrupt_template, 0, sizeof(interrupt_template));\n    interrupt_template.line = 0;\n    interrupt_template.name = interrupt_path;                        /*  \"<machine>.lca\"  */\n    interrupt_template.extra = d;\n    interrupt_template.interrupt_assert   = lca_interrupt_assert;\n    interrupt_template.interrupt_deassert = lca_interrupt_deassert;\n    interrupt_handler_register(&interrupt_template);\n\n    bus_isa_init(devinit->machine, interrupt_path,\n        BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1, LCA_ISA_BASE, LCA_ISA_MEMBASE);\n\n**The stubs are registered as the handlers for `<machine>.lca`, and `bus_isa_init` is handed THAT\nSAME PATH together with `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`.**  So the ISA bus -- both IDE controllers\nincluded -- routes its interrupts into a function whose entire body is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.  The\nfile's own comment above the stub says *\"Line 0 = ISA interrupt.\"*\n\n**WHY IT IS PLAUSIBLY THE WORST KILL IN THE FILE.**  Every other terminating call in `dev_lca.c`\nneeds a guest to write a value the model does not expect.  **This one needs only that an interrupt\nis asserted** -- i.e. ordinary disk activity on a machine whose DEVINIT attaches two IDE channels.\nNo unusual value, no undocumented register, no firmware revision.\n\n*** WHAT IS ESTABLISHED AND WHAT IS NOT -- read this before quoting the row. ***\n* **ESTABLISHED, by reading:** the stubs terminate unconditionally; they are registered as the\n  interrupt handlers; `bus_isa_init` receives the same interrupt path with both IDE channels.\n* **NOT ESTABLISHED:** that any guest actually reaches them.  **No measurement has been made.**  A\n  device could conceivably never assert on this path, and `interrupt_handler_register` semantics\n  have not been read here.  The row does NOT claim \"alphabook1 cannot boot\"; it claims the wiring\n  is present and the handler is a kill.\n\n**NEXT STEP IS A RUNG-3 ATTEMPT, and the apparatus already exists.**\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) already boots\nunmodified `alphabook1` under the debugger with a disk image attached and drives guest `ldq`/`stq`\nthrough real decode.  The question is whether a guest access can make an IDE channel assert while\nsingle-stepping -- **and the carrier's rule cuts against it: a step-based probe sees a SYNCHRONOUS\nassert (a device handler reached inside the guest's own store) but NOT one that depends on a\nhost-timer callback.**  So the honest first question is *what produces the assert*, and if the\nanswer is `timer_add`, this needs a free-running probe rather than a stepped one.\n\n**DISPOSITION: option-set MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT), and the set's other members do not apply.**  There\nis no value being refused and nothing to decline, accept, mask or fault -- it is an entire path\nstubbed out.  The carrier added member 7 on 2026-09-06 from this very file, using these very\nfunctions as its example.  A cheaper interim is member 3 (decline + LATCHED announce): return\nwithout asserting, announce once, and let the guest see a missing interrupt rather than a dead host\n-- **but that trades a host kill for a silently lost interrupt**, which is the readback-lie shape\n[[lcabadval]] is currently held on, so it is a design question and not an obvious win.\n\n**TRANSFERABLE PRECEDENT, already closed in this tree:** [[lunaabsentcpu]] -- *\"dev_luna88k.c\nreassert_interrupts() calls the NULL interrupt callback of an UNCONFIGURED cpu slot\"* -- same family\n(a terminating/absent interrupt callback reached by ordinary operation), fixed, and closed with a\nrung-3 probe witness (`regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`).  Read that round before designing this one.\n\nsev HIGH on the wiring; it would be CRITICAL if a witness lands, and the row says so rather than\npre-judging it.\n\n*** THE WITNESS-SHAPE QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND FAVOURABLY: THE ASSERT IS GUEST-DRIVEN, NOT\nHOST-TIMER-DRIVEN, SO A STEPPED PROBE CAN REACH IT. ***\n\nThe carrier demands one question before designing any probe around a tick: *what produces the thing\nthe tick consumes?*  If the answer is `timer_add`, the probe must free-run, because `debugger.c`\ncalls `timer_stop()` while interacting and `step N` returns before `timer_start()`.\n\n    DEVICE_TICK(wdc)\n    {\n            struct wdc_data *d = (struct wdc_data *) extra;\n\n            if (d->int_assert)\n                    INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n    }\n\n**What it consumes is `d->int_assert`, a flag the COMMAND PATH sets from an ordinary guest register\nwrite.**  No host timer produces it.  And tick functions do run while stepping -- `machine.c`\ndecrements `ticks_till_next[te]` by 1 under `single_step` instead of by `N_SAFE_DYNTRANS_LIMIT` --\nso the tick fires, one tick at a time.\n\n> **PREDICTED ROUTE: a guest write issues a wdc command that sets `int_assert`; the probe steps far\n> enough for the tick to run; `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq)` dispatches to the registered handler for\n> `<machine>.lca`; and that handler is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.**  Synchronous in the carrier's sense\n> -- what the tick consumes comes from the guest, not from SIGALRM.\n\n**STILL A PREDICTION, AND THREE LINKS ARE UNVERIFIED:** (1) that `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` on `d->irq`\nresolves to the LCA handler rather than to an intermediate ISA controller that swallows it; (2) how\nmany steps the tick needs; (3) whether `alphabook1` reaches a state where a wdc command can be\nissued from a cold debugger at all.\n\n**LINK (1) IS THE ONE TO READ FIRST.**  `bus_isa_init` may interpose its own interrupt controller\nbetween the IDE channel and the `<machine>.lca` path -- in which case the assert never reaches these\nstubs, and the row's severity drops sharply.  **If it interposes, THAT is the finding, not a\nfailure**, and the row should be re-graded rather than quietly dropped.\n\n*Recorded because it converts an open-ended witness question into three named, checkable links.*\n\n---\n\n*** A PROCESS NOTE, because it happened while writing THIS paragraph and it is the second instance\ntoday. ***  The first attempt to record the above went through `python -c '...'` and bash tore the\nsingle-quoted string apart on the apostrophes in \"guest's\" and \"carrier's\", exactly as it had done\nhours earlier on a different note.  **The rule minted from the first instance -- PROSE DESTINED FOR\nA RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT FILE, NEVER THROUGH `-c` -- was already written down in this same ledger\nwhen I broke it again.**  That is the carrier's own structure-versus-memory finding landing on me\ntwice in one session: knowing a rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems,\nand only the second is solved by writing more.  This paragraph was written from a file and landed\nintact on the first attempt.\n\n*** LINK (1) IS SETTLED BY READING, AND IT RAISES THE SEVERITY RATHER THAN LOWERING IT.  THE\nINTERPOSED PIC DOES NOT SWALLOW THE ASSERT -- IT FORWARDS IT, AND BOTH STUBS ARE REACHABLE. ***\n\nI flagged `bus_isa_init` as possibly interposing a controller that absorbs the interrupt.  **It does\ninterpose, and it forwards.**  The full chain, every link read:\n\n    dev_wdc.c    DEVICE_TICK(wdc):  if (d->int_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n                                    -- d->irq is \"<machine>.lca.isa.14\" (wdc0_irq = 14,\n                                       wdc1_irq = 15, bus_isa.c:180)\n\n    bus_isa.c    isa_interrupt_assert():  sets the PIC IRR bit for that line, then\n                                          isa_interrupt_common(d, old_isa_assert)\n\n    bus_isa.c    isa_interrupt_common():  cascades PIC2 -> PIC1 IRQ 2, then\n                                          new_isa_assert = d->pic1->irr & ~d->pic1->ier;\n                                          if (old == new) return;\n                                          if (!new) { INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq); return; }\n                                          ... otherwise asserts upward\n\n    dev_lca.c    lca_interrupt_assert() / lca_interrupt_deassert():\n                                          fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n\n**`d->irq` in `bus_isa_data` is the connection made at `bus_isa.c:193`:\n`INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path, d->irq)` -- and `DEVINIT(lca)` passes `interrupt_path` =\n`\"<machine>.lca\"` as that base path.  So the upward assert lands exactly on the two stubs.**\n\n**AND `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC` IS NOT SET BY LCA**, so the whole block at `bus_isa.c:190-209` runs:\nLCA passes only `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`.  The 16 ISA lines are registered and the upward\nconnection is made.\n\n*** BOTH DIRECTIONS KILL, WHICH I HAD NOT EXPECTED. ***  `isa_interrupt_common` calls\n**`INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq)`** at the point where the last unmasked IRR bit clears -- and\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` is the same bare `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.  So an ordinary interrupt LIFECYCLE\n-- assert, then acknowledge -- reaches a terminating stub at BOTH ends.  A fix that de-fatalises\nonly the assert leaves the deassert live.\n\n**THE ONE REMAINING GATE, and it is NOT a guest-unmask requirement in the direction I first\nassumed:** the upward assert fires when `pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier` becomes non-zero, i.e. when an IRR\nbit is set and NOT masked by `ier`.  **`ier` is the PIC's interrupt-ENABLE/mask register, and its\nRESET VALUE has NOT been read here** (`dev_8259.c`).  If it starts at 0 -- nothing masked -- then\nthe FIRST IDE assert propagates immediately with no guest setup at all.  **That is the next thing to\nread, and it decides whether this is \"reachable after ordinary driver setup\" or \"reachable\nimmediately\".**  Recorded as unread rather than guessed.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS HIGH AND IS NOT RAISED TO CRITICAL YET**, because reachability is still established\nby READING only -- no witness has been run.  What has changed is that the plausible-interposition\nescape hatch is now closed: the PIC forwards, and both stubs are on the path.\n\n*** THE LAST GATE IS OPEN AT RESET.  THE CHAIN IS NOW COMPLETE WITH EVERY LINK READ, AND IT NEEDS NO\nGUEST SETUP AT ALL. ***\n\nThe remaining question was the PIC mask: the upward assert fires when `pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier` goes\nnon-zero, so a non-zero reset `ier` would mask IDE and gate the whole path behind guest driver\nconfiguration.  Read:\n\n    dev_8259.c:266    memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct pic8259_data));\n    dev_8259.c:213    d->ier = idata;          <- the ONLY write to ier, from a guest store\n\n**`ier` is zeroed at init and is never set anywhere else.**  So at reset `ier == 0`, `irr & ~ier`\nis just `irr`, and **the FIRST set IRR bit propagates upward immediately.**  Nothing is masked until\na guest deliberately masks it.\n\n*** THE COMPLETE CHAIN, EVERY LINK READ IN THE SOURCE: ***\n\n    1.  dev_wdc.c      DEVICE_TICK(wdc): if (d->int_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n                       `int_assert` is set by the guest COMMAND path -- no host timer involved.\n                       d->irq is \"<machine>.lca.isa.14\"  (wdc0_irq = 14, wdc1_irq = 15)\n\n    2.  bus_isa.c      isa_interrupt_assert(): sets the PIC IRR bit, calls isa_interrupt_common()\n\n    3.  bus_isa.c      isa_interrupt_common(): new = pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier;  ier == 0 AT RESET,\n                       so `new` is non-zero on the first assert -> forwards upward on d->irq\n                       (and calls INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq) when the last unmasked bit clears)\n\n    4.  bus_isa.c:193  d->irq came from INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path, d->irq), and\n                       DEVINIT(lca) passes interrupt_path = \"<machine>.lca\" as that base path\n\n    5.  dev_lca.c:70   lca_interrupt_assert():   fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n        dev_lca.c:82   lca_interrupt_deassert(): fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n\n**So on `alphabook1`, an ordinary IDE interrupt ends the host process, and BOTH ends of the\ninterrupt lifecycle -- assert and deassert -- land on a terminating stub.**  No unusual register\nvalue, no undocumented offset, no firmware revision, and no guest PIC configuration: the mask is\nopen at reset.\n\n*** WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED, because this is the point at which overclaiming would be easy. ***\n* **ESTABLISHED BY READING, link by link:** the wiring, the forwarding, the open reset mask, and\n  that both stubs terminate unconditionally.\n* **NOT MEASURED.**  No probe has been run.  Nothing here shows that a guest on `alphabook1`\n  actually issues a wdc command that sets `int_assert`, nor that the tick runs before the guest\n  does something else.  **The row does NOT claim \"alphabook1 cannot boot\"** -- that is a plausible\n  consequence, not a finding, and it would need a witness.\n* The severity assessment therefore stays **HIGH on the wiring**.  It goes to the top of the\n  critical queue only when a witness lands, and the honest form of that sentence is what this row\n  says rather than what it implies.\n\n**A FIX MUST TAKE BOTH STUBS.**  De-fatalising only `lca_interrupt_assert` leaves\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` live on the same path, reached at acknowledge time -- which would convert\n\"dies on the first disk interrupt\" into \"dies on the first disk interrupt ACK\" and read as a fix in\nevery test that only asserts.\n\n*Found by following [[lcabadval]]'s own eleven-kill census, which had already named these two as\n\"plausibly the most severe of the eleven\" and which nothing had followed up.*\n\n*** CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN PRECEDENT CLAIM: [[lunaabsentcpu]] TRANSFERS AS A **WITNESS**\nTECHNIQUE AND **NOT** AS A DISPOSITION.  Checked by reading its diff rather than its title. ***\n\nThe row above says *\"read that round before designing this one\"*, which reads as though its FIX\ntransfers.  It does not.  `79cd12e`'s diff on `dev_luna88k.c` is a **BOUND** repair:\n\n    -   for (cpu = 0; cpu < MAX_CPUS; cpu++)\n    +   for (cpu = 0; cpu < d->ncpus; cpu++)          /*  #503  */\n\nplus a captured `ncpus` snapshot.  **luna's defect was walking into UNPOPULATED slots and calling a\ncallback that was the memset's NULL** -- a SIGSEGV from an absent handler.  **LCA's defect is a\nhandler that is PRESENT and deliberately terminates.**  Bounding a walk cannot help when the walk is\ncorrect and the destination is `exit(1)`; there is no unconnected slot here.\n\n**WHAT DOES TRANSFER, and it is the more useful half: HOW TO DRIVE AN INTERRUPT FROM GUEST STORES\nALONE.**  `luna_absentcpu_probe.py`'s witness is *\"a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1\nfollowed by a store to SOFT_INT1\"* -- **unmask, then trigger, both ordinary guest stores, no host\ntimer and no disk.**  That is exactly the shape a witness for this row needs, and it is why the\n`#503` round is worth reading.\n\n**The ISA analogue is not identical**, because there is no soft-interrupt register on this bus: the\nPIC mask is already open at reset (so the unmask step is free) but the TRIGGER has to come from a\ndevice asserting, which is what the reachability census now out to codex is enumerating.\n\n*This is the warrant-transfer caution in miniature: a precedent that is genuinely same-family for\none purpose (witness construction) can be wrong for another (disposition), and the row title looks\nidentical either way.  Read the diff, not the title.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE MOST SEVERE ITEM IN THIS FILE IS NOW A RUNG-3 WITNESS, AND IT TAKES\nFOUR GUEST INSTRUCTIONS. ***  `regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py`, artifact\n`regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw`, sentinel `LCAIRQ_DONE`.\n\n    lpt ctl      yes     yes     CONTROL: same store, same window, a non-asserting port   ok\n    kbc live     yes     yes     DEVICE SIGNATURE: dev_pckbc_access announced the command ok\n    kbc kill     dead    alive   <- THE DEFECT                                            FAIL\n    kbc attrib   yes     yes     ATTRIBUTION                                              ok\n      EVIDENCE: [ pckbc: write to CTL: aa 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 ]\n      EVIDENCE: lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n    LCAIRQ_RESULT=3/4\n\n**The whole program is four instructions** -- build the scratch pointer, load the port address, load\nthe value, store it -- on unmodified `alphabook1`, and `0xAA` is the STANDARD 8042 SELF-TEST\ncommand.  No disk image, no OS, no host timer, no driver setup, no unusual value.\n\n*** THE MATCHED CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES IT ATTRIBUTABLE, and it is the census that supplied it. ***\n`lpt ctl` performs the IDENTICAL store -- same value, same width, same K0SEG path, same ISA window\n-- one port away, at `B+0x378`, where `dev_lpt_tick` is EMPTY and `dev_lpt_access` never asserts.\nIt SURVIVES.  So the death is attributable to the DEVICE REACHED, not to storing into ISA space,\nnot to the address width, and not to the debugger path.\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE WERE CORRECTED BY THE codex ISA CENSUS BEFORE THIS WAS BUILT**, and the row\nwould have failed without both: the WDC controllers exist ONLY IF an IDE image is attached, so the\nIDE route this row was written around is unavailable to a probe attaching a memory file; and PIT/RTC\nasserts are HOST-TIMER driven and therefore invisible to a stepped probe, while PCKBC and the UARTs\nare synchronous with the guest access.  *The census cost one codex invocation and it chose the route.*\n\n*** SEVERITY: the wiring claim is now MEASURED, not read.  This is a guest-drivable host kill in four\ninstructions through a documented register. ***  The row's earlier caution -- \"severity stays HIGH on\nthe wiring; it goes to the top of the critical queue only when a witness lands\" -- is now discharged\non its own terms.\n\n**STILL NOT CLAIMED:** that any shipped guest issues an 8042 self-test at that moment.  The witness\nshows the path is open and ordinary, not that a particular OS walks it.\n\n**AND THE CONTRACT LIMIT IS THE SAME ONE grok NAMED ON THE SIBLING ROW:** this witnesses THIS\n`exit(1)`; it is NOT a detector of a repair.  \"Host survives\" would green for a silent return, for a\nlatched announce, and for an actual interrupt implementation alike.  **A fix round owes a row that\ndiscriminates those** -- and note the deassert stub is on the same path and would need its own.\n\n*** TREE-WIDE CENSUS: THIS IS THE ONLY TERMINATING INTERRUPT HANDLER IN THE FORK, 2 OF 70.  The\nsibling grep is discharged with a denominator, and it comes back BOUNDED. ***\n\n    distinct functions ASSIGNED as an interrupt handler   70\n    of those, bodies located                              70      (no blind spots)\n    whose body calls exit()                                2      BOTH in dev_lca.c\n        lca_interrupt_assert      dev_lca.c:70\n        lca_interrupt_deassert    dev_lca.c:82\n    registered handlers that do NOT terminate             68\n\n**So this is NOT a class -- it is the tree's single outlier**, and no sibling hunt is owed.\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS WAS RUN TWICE ON PURPOSE, KEYED TWO DIFFERENT WAYS, BECAUSE THE FIRST KEY WAS A\nCORRELATE. ***  Pass 1 matched functions whose NAME ends in `interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` and found\n**69**.  Pass 2 keyed on what actually DEFINES the class -- a function ASSIGNED to\n`.interrupt_assert` / `.interrupt_deassert` -- and found **70**.  **One registered handler does not\nfollow the naming convention and was invisible to the name-keyed pass.**  Both agree on the answer\n(2), but only the second could have been trusted to give it: this project's deriver rule is to key\non what makes a member a member, and a name is a correlate whose error is invisible because the\noutput is still a plausible number.\n\n*** THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FIX IS THE USEFUL HALF: THERE ARE 68 WORKING PRECEDENTS. ***  The codex\nfix brief asks (Q2) whether an in-tree shape can be copied.  The census answers it before the seat\ndoes -- 68 registered handlers deliver, latch, forward or ignore an interrupt without ending the\nprocess, so the question is not *whether* a non-terminating handler is possible on this\narchitecture but *which* of the 68 shapes suits an Alpha ISA line.\n\n**AND IT STRENGTHENS THE CASE THAT THESE TWO ARE UPSTREAM RESIDUE RATHER THAN A DECISION** -- the\nsame reading the option-set rule reached for `sgireunmapped`, where the sibling handlers in the same\nfile already declined and the arm under review was the file's own outlier.  Here the outlier is\n2-of-70 across the whole fork.\n\n*** THE codex FIX DESIGN LANDED AND IT SOLVES THE PROBLEM BOTH WITNESS CONTRACTS DECLARED OPEN.\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/codex_fix_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**DENOMINATOR: 36/36 registration call sites in 28 files, 35 assert/deassert pairs = 70 callbacks.\nThat INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCES this row's own census of 70**, by a different method (registration\nsites rather than assignment targets).  Two derivations, same number.\n\n*** 1.  MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT) IS BLOCKED AT THE DYNTRANS CORE, NOT AT THE DEVICE. ***\n`alpha_irq_interrupt_assert()` exists and sets `cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 1`.  **But \"the only\nreferences to Alpha's `irq_asserted` are the two assignments\"** -- nothing consumes it -- and\n`DYNTRANS_RUN_INSTR_DEF`'s interrupt-dispatch block has **ARM, M88K, MIPS, PPC and SH branches and\nNO ALPHA BRANCH**.  So even correct LCA wiring would deliver nothing to guest software.  **This\nindependently confirms the filed row [[irqassertedunread]]** -- \"alpha, i960 and riscv set\ncd.<arch>.irq_asserted with no consumer\" -- reached from the other direction.\n\nA minimal wiring step is still identified and is NOT sufficient on its own: give `lca_data` a\nconnected upstream `struct interrupt`, connect it to `devinit->interrupt_path` (which already\ndefaults to the bootstrap CPU in `device_add()`), and forward both levels.  **LCA currently stores\nno upstream interrupt connection at all**, and passing its own object to the CPU callback would be\nwrong because `extra` points at `lca_data` rather than the destination CPU.\n\n*** 2.  MY Q3 FRAMING WAS REFUTED: THERE IS NO HOUSE POLICY FOR THIS CASE. ***  I asked which\ndisposition \"this tree's own practice favours\".  The answer is that the precedents CONFLICT:\n\n    cpu_sh.c    sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert/deassert()   silent TODO bodies\n    dev_pcc2.c  reassert_interrupts()                 diagnoses, then exit(1)\n    dev_sh4.c   sh4_timer_tick()                      latched announce, then continues\n\n*\"The SH4 example supports bounded reporting, but it does not establish a universal disposition for\nan asserted delivery path.\"*  My brief presumed a settled practice; there is none.\n\n*** 3.  A DISPOSITION I HAD NOT NAMED, WITH AN IN-TREE PRECEDENT: DIAGNOSE AND STOP THE GUEST CPU. ***\nNot `exit(1)` (host process death) and not a silent or latched return (invisible loss), but an\nEMULATOR-LIMITATION STOP: `alpha_palcode()` already does exactly this for unsupported PAL calls --\ndiagnostic plus `cpu->running = 0`.  **This is an emulator limitation, not an architectural guest\nFAULT**, and the distinction is the point.\n\n**AND A CAUTION THAT WOULD HAVE MADE A NAIVE FIX SUBTLY WRONG:** *\"merely changing `running` inside a\ndevice callback does not itself unwind the current batched execution.\"*  `alpha_instr_call_pal()`\nhandles PAL stops by redirecting `next_ic` to `nothing_call`; a device implementation must arrange\nthe same unwind.  *That is the same class as the four-readers-proposed-`return` incident -- a\nplausible edit that skips machinery the enclosing control flow depends on.*\n\n*** 4.  THE DISCRIMINATING ROW, WHICH IS WHAT BOTH WITNESS CONTRACTS SAID WAS MISSING. ***\n**\"Stopped guest state, plus the reason -- NOT survival.\"**  Proposed acceptance row: starting from\na running CPU, an unsupported LCA assertion produces its specific diagnostic and leaves\n`cpu.running == false`; with debugger-at-end enabled the debugger stays responsive and subsequent\nsteps execute no guest instructions; the matched non-asserting control remains RUNNING.\n\n    diagnosed guest stop                              GREEN\n    silent return                                     RED -- CPU remains running\n    latched announce and return                       RED -- CPU remains running\n    forwarding only, or delivery to a resuming handler RED -- CPU remains running\n    the existing exit(1)                              RED -- debugger/process is gone\n\n**That discriminates all five candidates**, and the state is already observable: `cpu_new()` exposes\n`running` as a setting, `machine_run()` skips stopped CPUs, and `emul_run()` enters the debugger\nafter all machines stop when debugger-at-end is set.\n\n> **SO THE CONTRACT LIMIT ON BOTH LCA WITNESSES IS LIFTED -- for THIS disposition.**  Each says\n> \"host survives\" cannot tell the fix members apart, and that remains true; the answer is that the\n> oracle must read the CPU's RUNNING STATE and the diagnostic, not the host's survival.  The\n> existing probes would need that row added; neither has it today.\n\n**THE FABLE QUESTION IN §7j-1 IS NARROWED BUT NOT ANSWERED.**  I asked whether a latched decline is\nacceptable for an unimplemented delivery path.  codex's answer is that a THIRD option dominates both\nhorns -- but choosing between \"stop the guest with a diagnostic\" and \"lose interrupts quietly and\nkeep running\" is still a judgment about what an emulator owes its user, and that is exactly what the\nflagship seat is held for.  **§7j-2 (what observable discriminates) is now ANSWERED and can be\nstruck from the queue when the adjudication runs.**\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING CLAIM WAS VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, AND MY FIRST CHECK NEARLY REFUTED IT\nWRONGLY. ***\n\ncodex's whole disposition rests on \"the Alpha CPU cannot take an asynchronous interrupt\".  Checked:\n\n    grep -rn 'alpha.irq_asserted' src/     ->  2 hits, BOTH assignments in cpu_alpha.c\n    the dyntrans interrupt dispatch        ->  cpu_dyntrans.c tests cd.arm.irq_asserted (:208),\n                                               cd.m88k.irq_asserted (:212, :382),\n                                               cd.ppc.irq_asserted (:243) -- and NO cd.alpha.\n\n**CONFIRMED.**  Nothing consumes the Alpha flag and no dispatch branch exists for it.\n\n**MY FIRST CHECK WAS A CORRELATE AND IT FOUND A FALSE POSITIVE.**  I grepped\n`#if defined(DYNTRANS_ALPHA)` in `cpu_dyntrans.c` and got a hit at `:486` -- which is\n`n_args_to_print` in a DISASSEMBLY function and has nothing to do with interrupts.  Read as a\nrefutation, that hit would have produced a confident wrong correction to a seat that was right.\n**The fix was to search for the THING (an `irq_asserted` test) rather than for a correlate (the\narchitecture ifdef)** -- the deriver rule, on a one-line verification.\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** codex: CONFIRMED GUEST-REACHABLE PROCESS TERMINATION ON alphabook1, WITH THE PATH NAMED. ***\n\n    dev_ns16550_access() / scheduled UART tick -> dev_ns16550_tick()\n        -> isa_interrupt_assert() -> isa_interrupt_common() -> lca_interrupt_assert() -> exit(1)\n\n**The decisive wiring is one line after the registration:** `devinit_lca()` passes the same\ninterrupt path to `bus_isa_init()` (`dev_lca.c:418`), which connects its upstream interrupt to the\nsupplied template (`bus_isa.c:190`) and **unconditionally installs the first UART on ISA IRQ 4**\n(`bus_isa.c:250` -- verified by reading: the `ns16550 irq=%s.isa.%i` device_add with `4`).  No\nmachine-description modification is involved, which is what makes it a witness candidate rather\nthan laundering.\n\n**LCA line 0 is the AGGREGATE ISA input, not ISA IRQ 0** -- a correction to my brief's reading of\nthe comment.  And missing Alpha CPU dispatch does not prevent reaching the callback: *the process\nexits before CPU acceptance matters*.\n\n*** THE REPAIR IS EXPLICITLY PARTIAL, AND I VERIFIED THE REASON MYSELF. ***  codex says forwarding\nto an upstream `struct interrupt` is the minimum useful repair but cannot complete delivery,\nbecause the Alpha CPU callbacks only set and clear `cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted` and **that field\nhas no reader**.  Checked by census:\n\n    cd.alpha.irq_asserted   written cpu_alpha.c:218, :223   declared cpu_alpha.h:161   READ NOWHERE\n    cd.arm.irq_asserted     READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:208\n    cd.m88k.irq_asserted    READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:212 and :382\n    cd.ppc.irq_asserted     READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:243 and cpu_ppc.c:309\n\n**`cpu_dyntrans.c` has no Alpha arm at all.**  So a correct forwarding fix would set a flag nothing\nconsumes -- honest containment, not delivery.\n\n**FOOTBRIDGE IS THE CLOSEST IN-TREE ANALOGUE and it does NOT transfer literally:** it registers\n`interrupt->line` as a **bit mask** while LCA registers **index zero**, so a copied\n`status |= interrupt->line` would record nothing.\n\n*** MY OWN CITATIONS WERE CONTENTS ENTRIES, WHICH codex CAUGHT. ***  The brief cited manual lines\n`175` and `510` for the interrupt sections; those are TABLE-OF-CONTENTS rows.  The bodies are at\n`:4459` (S4.1.13) and `:10729` (S6.4.13).  *I had read the bodies and then cited the contents.*\n\n*** WITNESS ATTEMPT -- MADE, HARNESS WORKS, AND IT DID **NOT** FIRE.  Recorded as an attempt,\nwhich is what the lifecycle rule asks for. ***\n\n`_scratchpad/lcairq_probe.py`, harness copied from `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (pty.fork,\n`-V -x -E alpha -e alphabook1 -M 64 0x20000000:alpha_dummy.bin`).  Addresses derived, not guessed:\n`LCA_ISA_BASE = LCA_PCI_SIO(0x1c0000000) + 0x10000000`, UART at `+0x3f8`, K0SEG for the debugger.\n\n    live        alive   alive   CONTROL: plain step on an unmodified machine          ok\n    devsig      alive   alive   CONTROL: the UART window reads through the same decode ok\n    spin only   alive   alive   CONTROL: free-running self-branch, no UART setup       ok\n    irq assert  alive   died    IER=0x02, MCR=0x08, THR write, then `continue`    *** did not fire ***\n\n**Three controls green and the writes visibly land** (the debugger echoes each `put b`).  Two\nthings were learned and are worth carrying: the 8-byte all-zero dummy decodes as `call_pal\nPAL_halt` and **stops the machine before any device tick can run**, which is why the first attempt\nneeded a planted self-branch (`br r31,-1` = `0xc3ffffff`); and even free-running with IER, MCR and\na THR write in place, no assertion reached the stub.\n\n**THE OPEN QUESTION IS WHETHER A DEBUGGER `put` REACHES `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` AT ALL.**\n`dev_ns16550.c:353` registers via `memory_device_register`, and this project already records that\ndebugger access can bypass a handler depending on the registration flags.  If `put` writes a shadow\nrather than the device, `IIR_TXRDY` was never set and the tick's condition was never true -- which\nwould make the attempt inconclusive rather than negative.  **That is the next thing to settle, and\nit is a measurement, so it belongs to the reserved measuring seat rather than to another reading.**\n\n*So the row stands on codex's SOURCE-DERIVED reachability, not on an executed witness.  The\ndistinction matters and is recorded rather than blurred.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS ATTEMPT'S OPEN QUESTION IS NOW SETTLED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT MY APPARATUS COULD\nNOT DO WHAT I ASKED OF IT.  A DEBUGGER `put' NEVER REACHES `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)'. ***\n\n    debugger_cmds.c:1461, :1507   memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE, CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS)\n    memory_rw.c:584               if (!no_exceptions || (flags & DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS))\n                                          -> only THEN is devices[i].f() called\n    dev_ns16550.c:353-355         memory_device_register(..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)\n\n`DM_DEFAULT` is 0, so the UART carries no `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS` bit; the debugger passes\n`NO_EXCEPTIONS`; **the handler is skipped.**  My three `put b' writes to IER, MCR and THR were\nabsorbed without ever invoking `dev_ns16550_access`, so `IIR_TXRDY` was never set, the tick's\ncondition was never true, and nothing asserted.\n\n> **SO THE ATTEMPT WAS INCONCLUSIVE FOR AN APPARATUS REASON, AND THAT IS NOW A FACT RATHER THAN A\n> SUSPICION.**  It says nothing whatever about whether the defect is reachable -- codex's\n> source-derived path stands untouched.\n\n*** AND IT GENERALISES INTO A PROBE-WRITING RULE THIS PROJECT HAD ONLY HALF-RECORDED. ***  The\ncarrier already notes that the debugger's `dump` bypasses a `DM_DEFAULT` handler on READS, and\nprescribes guest loads for SH4 state.  **The same gate governs WRITES, by the same line of\n`memory_rw.c`** -- so:\n\n> **CONFIGURING A DEVICE FROM THE DEBUGGER WITH `put' IS A NO-OP AGAINST ANY `DM_DEFAULT' DEVICE.\n> Device access in a probe must be made by GUEST INSTRUCTIONS.**  `put' is for RAM -- scratch\n> pointers, planted code, page tables -- where no handler is involved.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PROBES ALREADY DO THIS CORRECTLY, which is why nothing else is broken:**\n`lca_confbus_probe.py` uses `put d' only to seed SCRATCH in RAM and then reaches the device with\nguest `STQ`/`LDQ`; `alpha_swpctx_v0_probe.py` (#514) uses `put' only for RAM and `call_pal', and\ntouches no device at all.  **My failed attempt is the one that broke the pattern**, and it broke it\nby configuring a device the debugger cannot configure.\n\n**WHAT THE WITNESS NOW NEEDS, concretely:** the UART's IER/MCR/THR must be written by guest stores.\nThe registers are byte-wide at `LCA_ISA_BASE + 0x3f8` with `addrmult = 1`, so this needs either a\nbyte store (`STB`, which is a BWX instruction and may not exist on a 21064-class part -- CHECK\nbefore assuming) or a wider store whose other bytes are harmless.  **That check is the next step\nand it is cheap; it is recorded here rather than guessed.**\n\n*** THE WITNESS FIRES.  `regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py', measured on `2ab3d2f' with `src/'\nUNMODIFIED: 2/4, and the transcript's last line is the stub's own diagnostic. ***\n\n    live         alive   alive   CONTROL: a free-running self-branch alone survives        ok\n    ram ctl      alive   alive   ATTRIBUTION: the IDENTICAL sequence against RAM survives   ok\n    uart irq     died    alive   an ordinary UART interrupt must not end the emulator     FAIL\n    stub named   yes     no      the stub's own TODO diagnostic must not appear           FAIL\n\n    GXemul> continue\n    lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n\n**THREE GUEST BYTE STORES END THE EMULATOR** -- IER `0x02`, MCR `0x08`, then any byte to THR --\non an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` with no `device_add` and no machine-description change.\n`WITNESS_HEAD_20260908.out` and the full session transcript are preserved beside it.\n\n*** `ram ctl' IS WHAT MAKES THIS A WITNESS RATHER THAN A CRASH REPORT. ***  It runs the\n**identical instruction sequence** -- same registers, same opcodes, same count -- against RAM\ninstead of the UART, and survives.  So the termination is attributable to the DEVICE and not to the\ncode sequence, the self-branch, or the free-running mode.  One control, two hypotheses separated.\n\n*** IT TOOK THREE FACTS TO GET HERE, AND TWO OF THEM WERE MINE TO GET WRONG. ***\n\n1. **codex's source path**, which named the wiring end to end: `devinit_lca()` hands its interrupt\n   path to `bus_isa_init()`, which unconditionally installs the first ns16550 on ISA IRQ 4.\n2. **The `put'/DM_DEFAULT finding** -- my FIRST attempt configured the UART with debugger `put' and\n   nothing happened, because `put' passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS' and `memory_rw' then skips a `DM_DEFAULT'\n   device's handler entirely.  *That attempt was inconclusive for an apparatus reason, and until it\n   was traced it looked like evidence the path was unreachable.*\n3. **`stb' is ungated in this tree.**  The UART registers are byte-wide, so the guest needs a byte\n   store -- a BWX instruction a real 21066 would not have.  `cpu_alpha_instr.c:895` decodes\n   `case 0x0e` with **no feature check**, and `ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX` (`cpu_alpha.h:57`) is **DEFINED\n   AND NEVER CONSULTED** -- the third declared-but-unused constant found in this header today,\n   after `ALPHA_PTE_ASM` and `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM`.  **So this probe depends on an emulator fact rather\n   than a hardware one, and a future round that gates BWX would break it.**  Recorded in the probe\n   header, not left to be rediscovered.\n\n**SEVERITY: the row is already `high` and this does not raise it -- it removes the last doubt about\nreachability.**  The witness ladder is explicit that rung measures reachability-proof strength and\nnever harm, so a witness landing does not by itself move severity.  *What it does change is that\n\"guest-reachable\" is no longer source-derived.*\n\n**WHAT THE ROW STILL OWES is unchanged and is the hard part:** the disposition.  Forwarding cannot\ncomplete delivery, because `cd.alpha.irq_asserted` has no reader and `cpu_dyntrans.c` has no Alpha\narm -- so any repair converts *\"the emulator terminates\"* into *\"the interrupt is silently\ndropped\"*.  That question is with codex and is queued for the flagship seat.\n\n*** CORRECTION, AND IT IS MINE: THIS ROW WAS ALREADY WITNESSED AND I BUILT A SECOND WITNESS\nWITHOUT CHECKING.  I ALSO OVERWROTE THE COMMITTED PROBE FILE DOING IT. ***\n\n`regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py` shipped in **`7c2f8e4` on 2026-09-07** -- *\"lcairqstubkill\nWITNESSED: four guest instructions end the host through a documented 8042 register\"* -- scoring\n**3/4**, with an LPT control chosen from a codex ISA census because `dev_lpt_tick` is empty and\ncannot assert.  **The ledger's own `witness` field said all of that**, and I replaced it with a\nthinner sentence about my own probe.\n\n**HOW IT WAS CAUGHT: the probe-wiring checker's DUPLICATE-KEY guard.**  A dict literal keeps only\nthe last value and says nothing, so the checker refuses two entries for one key -- and it named\n`lca_irqstub_probe.py`.  Without that guard I would have shipped a manifest that silently\ndescribed the wrong probe.  *Nothing else in the gate would have noticed, because the file existed\nand the row had a witness either way.*\n\n**DAMAGE, checked rather than assumed:** the probe file was overwritten and is **restored from\nHEAD**; the ledger `witness` field is **restored and extended**.  **Yesterday's artifacts are\nintact** -- `witness_HEAD_20260907.raw` is unmodified, and `git diff HEAD` over that directory\nshows only my new files added.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS WORTH KEEPING:** my probe is a **SECOND, INDEPENDENT PATH** -- ns16550\non IRQ 4 rather than 8042 on IRQ 1, three byte stores rather than one word store, with its own\nidentical-sequence-against-RAM control.  **Two independent guest routes to one stub is real\nvalue**: a repair that silences one and not the other is visible at once.  It is renamed\n`lca_irqstub_uart_probe.py` and its artifacts `UART_WITNESS_*`, so nothing can be mistaken for the\nrow's witness of record.\n\n**AND TWO FINDINGS FROM BUILDING IT STAND ON THEIR OWN**, independent of the duplication: the\n`put`/`DM_DEFAULT` mechanism (a debugger `put` never reaches a `DM_DEFAULT` device's handler, now\nfiled as a probe-writing hazard), and that `ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX` is defined and never consulted so\n`stb` executes on any Alpha here.\n\n> **THE PROCESS FAILURE IS EXACT AND WORTH NAMING: I read the row's `state_note` and its briefs,\n> and never read its `witness` FIELD.**  The standing instruction is to check the repo before\n> starting, and I checked the prose and not the schema.  *A row that already carries a witness is\n> the cheapest possible thing to check, and it is one field.*\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION IS DESIGNED, WITH TWO TRANSFERRED WARRANTS AND AN EXPLICIT LIMIT. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *bounded announcement plus level forwarding, as a PARTIAL repair, with Alpha\ninterrupt delivery left explicitly unresolved.*\n\n*** IT ARGUED THE STRONGEST CASE AGAINST THAT BEFORE HOLDING IT, which is what was asked. ***\n*\"It preserves the guest's failure while weakening its visibility.  An interrupt-dependent guest\ncan still hang; a warning can disappear into logs; an automation wrapper can mistake continued\nprocess life for success.  Forwarding adds no presently functioning guest capability because\nnothing consumes the flag.  An announced drop would achieve the same immediate containment with\nless plumbing.\"*\n\n> **AND THEN THE DISCRIMINATION THAT SETTLES IT:** *\"That objection defeats a claim of FIXED ALPHA\n> INTERRUPTS.  It does not defeat the narrower improvement of removing these two process exits.\"*\n> The round may remove the terminations; it may not claim delivery.\n\n**THE ADDITION LICENCE IS SATISFIED BY TWO NAMED IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, not by \"it cannot hurt\":**\nFootbridge's connection-and-propagation shape (`dev_footbridge.c:794`), and -- the one that matters\nfor the announce -- **`dev_pcc2.c:369`, which ALREADY ANNOUNCES AN UNAVAILABLE INTERRUPT-DELIVERY\nMODE ONCE.**  That is this exact disposition, already shipped elsewhere in the tree.\n\n**THE MINIMAL IMPLEMENTATION, concretely:** add `struct interrupt irq;` and `int\nirq_delivery_warned;` to `lca_data` (the existing `memset` initialises the latch);\n`INTERRUPT_CONNECT(devinit->interrupt_path, d->irq)` before creating downstream devices, since that\npath already defaults to the bootstrap CPU; a `lca_interrupt_notice()` helper carrying the `(once)`\nmarker; then `notice(); INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);` and the deassert mirror.  **N = 1 per instance\nwith an explicit suppression marker -- the house idiom, not the counter form.**\n\n*** IT ALSO CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER FOUR-ROW COVERAGE TABLE as having \"overstated what could\nimmediately become passing coverage\", which is a seat being harder on itself than on me: ***\n\n    callback forwarding      BUILDABLE TODAY as a host-side test; the current callbacks fail it\n                             and the small repair passes it\n    masking and release      SPLIT.  Level retention/withdrawal passes after the small repair;\n                             masked-versus-unmasked ENTRY requires CPU delivery, and no entry in\n                             either case proves nothing about masking\n    vector acknowledgement   BLOCKED on missing LCA acknowledgement support -- a SEPARATE missing\n                             dependency, implementable independently of CPU dispatch\n    guest execution          CANNOT pass with forwarding alone\n\n**SO THE ROUND'S DETECTOR IS THE FIRST ROW PLUS HALF THE SECOND**, and the record must say that\nrather than implying the table.\n\n*** AND THE DELIVERY ROUND IS BIGGER THAN A CONDITIONAL IN `cpu_dyntrans.c'. ***  codex found two\nmore declared-but-unused things in this layer: **the tree WRITES `kentry[]` AND NEVER CONSUMES\nIT**, and **`PAL_OSF1_rti` has a printable name with no execution implementation** -- so the PAL\ndispatcher cannot supply the interrupt entry/return lifecycle at all.  *That is the fifth\ndeclared-but-unused Alpha item found today, after `ALPHA_PTE_ASM`, `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and\n`ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX`.*  Queued as its own compatibility round with a named measured delta: on the\npartial-repair baseline, an eligible asserted input leaves a guest handler counter unchanged, and\nthe implementation makes the handler execute and return.\n\n**Footbridge supplies no Alpha PAL entry convention**, so that warrant does NOT transfer to the\ndelivery round -- only to the propagation shape.\n\n*** THE SIBLING CHECK THE ERROR DEMANDED, AND IT CAME BACK CLEAN. ***  Having overwritten one\ncommitted witness, the obvious question is whether I have done it anywhere else, or whether any\nother row's `witness' field names something that is no longer there.  Censused across every OPEN\nrow:\n\n    open rows                                        194\n    of which ALREADY CARRY A WITNESS                 119\n    whose witness names a MISSING file                 0\n\n**Zero.**  Every path named in a witness field exists on disk, so this was a single incident and\nthe restore closed it.  *And all EIGHT high-severity open rows already carry a witness* -- which\nsays something about the queue that is worth writing down: **the top of this queue is not short of\nevidence, it is short of DISPOSITIONS.**  That is the shape of work the flagship adjudication is\nfor, and it is why the packet's questions are disposition questions rather than requests for more\nmeasurement.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION QUESTION IS SETTLED, 2026-09-08, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT FORWARDING IS A NO-OP ON\nTHIS ARCHITECTURE.  Led by the codex seat, VERIFIED BY ME IN ONE GREP. ***\n\nThe round had been parked on *\"forwarding alone leaves Alpha delivery unresolved\"* without anyone\nestablishing what Alpha delivery actually IS.  A codex ANALYSIS job (see `codexcyberflag` -- the\nconstruction-shaped brief is refused, the analysis-shaped one is not) said mid-stream:\n\n> *\"Alpha has a CPU interrupt pin and an `irq_asserted` field, but the searches so far show only\n> WRITES to that field.\"*\n\n**Its stream then ended without a final verdict, so this would have been an UNREAD REVIEW had the\n`.err` not been opened.  Checked rather than taken:**\n\n    grep -rn irq_asserted src/          ->  57 references, classified\n\n    cpu_alpha.c:218    cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 1;      WRITE\n    cpu_alpha.c:223    cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 0;      WRITE\n    cpu_alpha.h:161    int irq_asserted;                    DECL\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    READS OF cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted ANYWHERE IN src/ :   ZERO\n\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` is where a pending interrupt becomes a delivered one, and it tests exactly three\narchitectures -- `cd.arm.irq_asserted` (`:208`), `cd.m88k.irq_asserted` (`:212`, `:382`) and\n`cd.ppc.irq_asserted` (`:243`).  **Alpha is absent from that list.**  `cpu_ppc.c:309` shows the\nsame field being consumed on the PPC side; there is no Alpha counterpart.\n\n> **SO THE ALPHA CPU MODEL HAS AN INTERRUPT-PENDING FLAG THAT NOTHING CONSUMES.  Wiring the LCA\n> stubs to `cpu_interrupt()` would set a field no code reads: a NO-OP, not a delivery.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS SETTLES, and it changes the round from blocked to shippable: ***\n\n* **Member 7 (IMPLEMENT) is NOT a wiring job.**  It requires adding Alpha interrupt dispatch to\n  `cpu_dyntrans.c` plus the PALcode entry and IPL check that go with it -- a SUBSYSTEM, and its own\n  round.  The earlier note's instinct that *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete\"*\n  was right about the machine and wrong about the size of the gap: the machine is already\n  incomplete, before this row's stubs are reached.\n* **The partial repair is COHERENT, which was the open question.**  Nothing in the model blocks\n  waiting for an acknowledgement that cannot come, because nothing is waiting at all -- there is no\n  consumer.  Replacing `exit(1)` with a bounded announce leaves the guest with interrupts silently\n  dropped, which is EXACTLY what it already gets on every Alpha path that does not terminate.\n* **The honest disclosure the round owes** is therefore not *\"we chose not to forward\"* but\n  *\"Alpha interrupt delivery does not exist in this CPU model; these stubs cannot forward to\n  anything, and the fix removes a host termination without pretending to add delivery.\"*\n\n**AND IT REFRAMES THE SEVERITY HONESTLY IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.**  The termination is still\nguest-reachable and still witnessed twice (8042 command port, 3/4; ns16550 IRQ 4, 2/4), so the row\nstays HIGH.  But the fix buys *the host stops dying*, NOT *interrupts start working* -- and a round\nblock claiming the latter would be a wrong record.\n\n*Provenance worth keeping: this is the reading-seat-buys-the-class pattern exactly.  codex named the\nfamily (`only writes to that field`) and never finished its answer; one grep turned that into a\nmeasured fact that settles a design question two panels had left open.  The cost of NOT opening a\nzero-byte job's `.err` would have been another round of design argument.*\n\n*** PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  P4 SURVIVED A SEAT THAT TRIED TO REFUTE IT, AND FOUR OF MY BRIEF'S OTHER\nCLAIMS DID NOT. ***\n\n**grok: \"RATIFY member 3 (per-instance, two bits), do not forward, do not call this delivery.\nP4 holds and is STRONGER than the brief states.\"**  On Q1 specifically: *\"Yes. I tried to refute it\nand failed.  Delivery does not exist.\"*  **deepseek** conditionally accepted and did the correct\npacket-fed thing -- refused to ratify P4 as unverifiable from the packet and named the exact search\nthat settles it, rather than agreeing.\n\n*** THE FOUR CORRECTIONS, three of which change the round. ***\n\n**(1) Q2 WAS MIS-FRAMED AND THE QUESTION CANNOT BE ASKED.**  I asked whether the stubs should write\n`irq_asserted` anyway.  grok: *\"`interrupt->extra` is `struct lca_data *`, NOT a `struct cpu *`.\nThere is no `irq_asserted` in this file to write.  Forwarding is `INTERRUPT_CONNECT(cpu->path, ...)`\nplus `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`, i.e. CHIPSET WIRING, not a field store.\"*  So the option I was weighing does\nnot exist at these call sites at all -- **the answer is not \"no, don't\", it is \"there is nothing\nthere to write.\"**\n\n**(2) \"A DRIVER WILL USUALLY REACH BOTH WITHIN ONE INTERRUPT CYCLE\" IS FALSE OF THIS CODE, and I\nused it to argue for two latch bits.**  grok traced it: *\"`isa_interrupt_common` takes ONE EDGE per\ncall -- ASSERT if IRR went 0->nonzero, DEASSERT if it went nonzero->0, return if the aggregate\nstayed asserted.  The 8042 probe never reads the `0x55` reply, so it never deasserts.  The UART probe\nleaves IER on and free-runs, so tick keeps asserting.  **Existing witnesses do not touch\n`_deassert`.**\"*\n> **THE CONSEQUENCE IS COVERAGE DEBT AND IT IS DISCLOSED: the deassert latch is UNWITNESSED.**  Two\n> bits remain the right choice -- for the opposite reason to the one I gave -- because the two\n> callbacks are reached on DIFFERENT edges and a shared bit would report whichever arrived first.\n\n**(3) `detector:` AS THE WITNESS TERM IS A CATEGORY ERROR.**  I proposed it in Q5.  grok: *\"That term\nis for a fix with a measured detector and NO guest-path witness.  This row already has TWO rung-3\nprobes.  Unobservable DELIVERY is a CONTRACT fact, not a missing witness of the kill.\"*  Correct --\nthe round witnesses the TERMINATION, which is what it fixes; it does not claim to witness delivery,\nwhich it does not add.  The witness term stays `probe:`.\n\n**(4) MY §C0 COUNT WAS STALE** -- see `pass1_20260908/CORRECTION_C0.md`, written before this.  grok's\nframing adds the general rule: *\"Do not treat a DIRTY-TREE `grep -c` as the ship check.\"*\n\n*** (5) THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A BROKEN ROUND, AND IT NEEDS MEASUREMENT NOT READING. ***\n\ngrok: *\"The UART probe's `stub named` row is HOSTILE to member 3.  It wants `lca_interrupt_assert`\nABSENT.  A latched `fatal(\"...TODO\")` keeps that row RED FOREVER.  Drop or re-pin it under the\noracle-correction rule; do not ship member 3 against that want.\"*\n\n**The row is exactly as described:**\n`row(\"stub named\", \"no\" if \"lca_interrupt_assert\" not in t3 else \"yes\", \"no\", ...)`\n\n**BUT THE HAZARD MAY NOT REACH MY IMPLEMENTATION, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE MESSAGE TEXT.**  grok\nreasoned from a generic *latched `fatal(\"...TODO\")`* -- keeping the original wording, which contains\nthe function name.  The announce I wrote says *\"[ lca: ISA interrupt assert not implemented (Alpha\nhas no interrupt delivery); ignored  (once) ]\"* -- **\"ISA interrupt assert\", not\n`lca_interrupt_assert`** -- so the forbidden literal may never appear.\n\n> *** THAT IS A READING OF MY OWN STRING AND IT IS EXACTLY THE KIND OF CLAIM THIS PROJECT REQUIRES\n> TO BE MEASURED.  The post-fix run settles it; nothing is claimed here. ***  And whichever way it\n> lands, grok has identified a REAL DESIGN CONSTRAINT that was not in my brief and that I satisfied\n> by accident rather than by intent: **the announce must not contain the literal\n> `lca_interrupt_assert`, or it pins a shipped witness row red forever.**\n\n*** THE FIX IS IMPLEMENTED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-08.  ONE INSTRUMENT, BOTH DIRECTIONS. ***\n\n                                     PRE-FIX   POST-FIX\n    lca_irqstub_probe.py               3/6       6/6\n    lca_irqstub_uart_probe.py          2/5       5/5\n\nScratch clone `/tmp/gxlca`, baseline `git show HEAD:src/devices/dev_lca.c` rebuilt in the clone.\nNo project tree mutated.  Raws + a README naming which instrument produced which figure:\n`regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/fix_20260908/`.\n\n**THE DIFF:** both stubs keep a diagnostic and lose the termination -- option-set member 3, DECLINE\nplus a bounded announce.  **PER-INSTANCE** via `interrupt->extra`, not function-static, so one guest\naccess to one device instance cannot silence every other instance ([[staticlatchscope]]).  **TWO\nseparate latch bits**, because grok established the two callbacks are reached on DIFFERENT EDGES --\n`isa_interrupt_common` takes one edge per call -- so a shared bit would report whichever arrived\nfirst and hide the other entirely.\n\n**THREE ROWS ADDED, because both probes' own contracts demanded them.**  `lca_irqstub_probe.py`'s\nheader says: *\"it is NOT a detector of a repair: 'host survives' would green for a silent return, for\na latched announce, and for an actual interrupt implementation alike.  A fix round owes a row that\ndiscriminates those.\"*\n\n    irq announce   the announce text is present -- a silent return prints nothing, and a REAL\n                   implementation would print nothing either, so only this repair prints this\n    irq honest     the announce contains \"Alpha has no\" -- the round's claim is that it stops the\n                   host dying WITHOUT making interrupts work, and this is the only place a reader\n                   ever learns that\n    irq latched    COUNTS the announce in the free-running UART session, want exactly 1.  That\n                   session's tick re-asserts repeatedly, so an unlatched announce prints many times.\n                   Counting is the oracle because a PRESENCE row would be satisfied by a latch that\n                   never engages -- the very mutant it exists to catch.\n\n*** A DEFECT I INTRODUCED WHILE ADDING THEM, AND CAUGHT BY THE PROBE'S OWN OUTPUT. ***  The rows went\nin BELOW each score computation, splitting both scorers: numerator over the old row set, denominator\nevaluated at print time.  **The 8042 probe printed SIX ROWS ALL `ok` UNDER A SUMMARY OF 4/6.**  The\nUART probe reported 3/5 pre-fix where the honest score was 2/5 -- and post-fix it rendered 5/5\nCORRECTLY BY COINCIDENCE, because nothing failed.  Repaired in both files by moving the computation\nadjacent to the print.  A census over HEAD shows **0 of 73** candidate probes were ever split, so\nboth instances were mine; filed as [[splitscorer]] (low) with that correction.\n\n**KNOWN GAP, DISCLOSED IN THE PROBE UNDER CLAUSE (e): `lca_interrupt_deassert` IS WHOLLY\nUNWITNESSED.**  Neither witness reaches the deassert edge -- the 8042 probe never reads the `0x55`\nreply, and the UART probe leaves IER on and free-runs, so the aggregate never falls back to zero.\nIts latch bit and its whole body are uncovered and no mutant of them could be killed.  Closing it\nneeds a guest sequence that LOWERS the interrupt, which is a new session rather than a new row.  Put\nto pass 2 as an explicit question rather than shipped silently.\n\n*** PASS 2: grok returned NOT RATIFY, AND A MUTANT CONFIRMED IT.  My best row was vacuous. ***\n\n**Its verdict:** *\"The disposition in `dev_lca.c` is the right one.  The 11-row instrument does not\ndefend it.  Two floor holes, both from ONE false premise: that UART ticks re-enter\n`lca_interrupt_assert`.\"*\n\n**IT READ THE PATH INSTEAD OF THE BRIEF:** *\"`isa_interrupt_common` is EDGE-TRIGGERED on the PIC\naggregate.  If the UART never drops IRR, `irq latched` is vacuous.\"*\n\n*** MEASURED, NOT CONCEDED.  Mutant `M1_unlatch` removes the latch so the announce fires on EVERY\nentry: ***\n\n    base           kbc=6/6   uart=5/5   announce_count=1\n    M1_unlatch     kbc=6/6   uart=5/5   announce_count=1     *** CLEAN SWEEP ***\n\n**The count is 1 with and without the latch, because the stub is entered exactly ONCE however long\nthe guest free-runs.**  My `irq latched` row could not vary under the only mutant it existed to\ncatch.\n\n> **THIS IS THE CHANNEL FAILURE FOR THE FOURTH TIME IN TWO ROUNDS, AND THE SAME MISTAKE EVERY TIME:\n> I named an observable by asking what the code prints on the path I HAD IN MIND, never by tracing\n> what the MUTANT actually reaches.**  Here I assumed re-entry; the caller is edge-triggered.\n\n**DISPOSITION -- RELABELLED, NOT DELETED, and the distinction is the honest part.**  The row is\nvacuous *as a latch test* and is NOT vacuous *as a statement about behaviour*: the announce really is\nemitted once, and pinning that catches a future edit moving it outside the guard.  It is now\n`irq once`, its note says it does NOT test the latch, and the measurement is quoted beside it.\n\n*** AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS A GAP THIS ROUND CANNOT CLOSE: THE LATCH IS UNEXERCISED BY ANY WITNESS\nIN THIS TREE. ***  Exercising it needs TWO assert edges in one session -- an IRR that falls and rises\nagain -- which is the SAME missing capability as the unwitnessed deassert path.  So the round ships\na latch it cannot test, and says so under clause (e) rather than implying coverage.\n\n*** A SECOND CORRECTION FROM grok, and it was about to ship in PRODUCT CODE. ***  My comment said\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` delivers *\"for ARM, M88K and PPC only\"*.  Checked: it delivers for **FIVE** --\nARM (`:208`), M88K (`:212`), MIPS via the COP0 exception model (`:219`), PPC (`:243`) and SH via\n`int_to_assert` (`:247`).  **I had enumerated by grepping `irq_asserted`, which is a CORRELATE of\ndelivering rather than the property**, and missed the two that deliver by another mechanism.  *The\nfourth correlate-not-the-property error of the day.*  **The Alpha conclusion is unchanged and\nSTRENGTHENED -- it is the outlier among five, not three** -- and the comment now says so with the\nreason, because a wrong reason gets reused.\n\n**grok also CONFIRMED what it could:** P4 holds (two writes of `cd.alpha.irq_asserted`, no reads, no\n`DYNTRANS_ALPHA` arm); `interrupt->extra` is a `struct lca_data *` so forwarding is chipset wiring;\nand my Q1 reasoning -- the predicates that gated death are the EMPTY SET, because the stubs had no\n`if` at all -- is right.\n\n**AND IT WAS EXPLICIT ABOUT WHAT IT DID NOT DO:** *\"The scores 6/6 and 5/5 -- I did not re-run the\nprobes.  I read the artifacts.  Those logs match the brief.  They do NOT show an unlatch mutant.\"*\nThat last sentence is the whole finding, stated as a gap in the evidence rather than as an\naccusation, and it was correct.\n\n**FIX SHIPPED 2026-09-09 as `#516` (74daefd)**: both stubs de-fatalised, 12-row detector, tables G+H (16 cells, 14 killed, 2 measured equivalent), chain closed on pass 3.  State `in work` until the regress phase runs on it.",
+   "state_note": "[2026-09-19] SHIPPED as #516 (74daefd, 2026-09-09); its repair chain closed on pass 3.  Awaiting its regress/closure stage (Fable-only by standing directive).  FILED 2026-09-07.  **NOT a new discovery of the SITE** -- [[lcabadval]]'s eleven-kill census already\nenumerated `:73` and `:85` and called them *\"plausibly the most severe of the eleven\"*.  What was\nmissing is a ROW: the census names them inside a row titled for VALUE GUARDS, which is the census's\nown closing lesson (*a row named after one class hides the members of the others*), and\n`OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` mentioned them **zero** times.\n\n**THE SITE, verbatim -- there is no predicate at all:**\n\n    void lca_interrupt_assert(struct interrupt *interrupt)\n    {\n            fatal(\"lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n    void lca_interrupt_deassert(struct interrupt *interrupt)\n    {\n            fatal(\"lca_interrupt_deassert: TODO\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n*** THE WIRING, DERIVED BY READING `DEVINIT(lca)` -- AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THIS SEVERE. ***\n\n    memset(&interrupt_template, 0, sizeof(interrupt_template));\n    interrupt_template.line = 0;\n    interrupt_template.name = interrupt_path;                        /*  \"<machine>.lca\"  */\n    interrupt_template.extra = d;\n    interrupt_template.interrupt_assert   = lca_interrupt_assert;\n    interrupt_template.interrupt_deassert = lca_interrupt_deassert;\n    interrupt_handler_register(&interrupt_template);\n\n    bus_isa_init(devinit->machine, interrupt_path,\n        BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1, LCA_ISA_BASE, LCA_ISA_MEMBASE);\n\n**The stubs are registered as the handlers for `<machine>.lca`, and `bus_isa_init` is handed THAT\nSAME PATH together with `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`.**  So the ISA bus -- both IDE controllers\nincluded -- routes its interrupts into a function whose entire body is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.  The\nfile's own comment above the stub says *\"Line 0 = ISA interrupt.\"*\n\n**WHY IT IS PLAUSIBLY THE WORST KILL IN THE FILE.**  Every other terminating call in `dev_lca.c`\nneeds a guest to write a value the model does not expect.  **This one needs only that an interrupt\nis asserted** -- i.e. ordinary disk activity on a machine whose DEVINIT attaches two IDE channels.\nNo unusual value, no undocumented register, no firmware revision.\n\n*** WHAT IS ESTABLISHED AND WHAT IS NOT -- read this before quoting the row. ***\n* **ESTABLISHED, by reading:** the stubs terminate unconditionally; they are registered as the\n  interrupt handlers; `bus_isa_init` receives the same interrupt path with both IDE channels.\n* **NOT ESTABLISHED:** that any guest actually reaches them.  **No measurement has been made.**  A\n  device could conceivably never assert on this path, and `interrupt_handler_register` semantics\n  have not been read here.  The row does NOT claim \"alphabook1 cannot boot\"; it claims the wiring\n  is present and the handler is a kill.\n\n**NEXT STEP IS A RUNG-3 ATTEMPT, and the apparatus already exists.**\n`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (shipped 2026-09-07 for [[lcaconfbus1kill]]) already boots\nunmodified `alphabook1` under the debugger with a disk image attached and drives guest `ldq`/`stq`\nthrough real decode.  The question is whether a guest access can make an IDE channel assert while\nsingle-stepping -- **and the carrier's rule cuts against it: a step-based probe sees a SYNCHRONOUS\nassert (a device handler reached inside the guest's own store) but NOT one that depends on a\nhost-timer callback.**  So the honest first question is *what produces the assert*, and if the\nanswer is `timer_add`, this needs a free-running probe rather than a stepped one.\n\n**DISPOSITION: option-set MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT), and the set's other members do not apply.**  There\nis no value being refused and nothing to decline, accept, mask or fault -- it is an entire path\nstubbed out.  The carrier added member 7 on 2026-09-06 from this very file, using these very\nfunctions as its example.  A cheaper interim is member 3 (decline + LATCHED announce): return\nwithout asserting, announce once, and let the guest see a missing interrupt rather than a dead host\n-- **but that trades a host kill for a silently lost interrupt**, which is the readback-lie shape\n[[lcabadval]] is currently held on, so it is a design question and not an obvious win.\n\n**TRANSFERABLE PRECEDENT, already closed in this tree:** [[lunaabsentcpu]] -- *\"dev_luna88k.c\nreassert_interrupts() calls the NULL interrupt callback of an UNCONFIGURED cpu slot\"* -- same family\n(a terminating/absent interrupt callback reached by ordinary operation), fixed, and closed with a\nrung-3 probe witness (`regress/luna_absentcpu_probe.py`).  Read that round before designing this one.\n\nsev HIGH on the wiring; it would be CRITICAL if a witness lands, and the row says so rather than\npre-judging it.\n\n*** THE WITNESS-SHAPE QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND FAVOURABLY: THE ASSERT IS GUEST-DRIVEN, NOT\nHOST-TIMER-DRIVEN, SO A STEPPED PROBE CAN REACH IT. ***\n\nThe carrier demands one question before designing any probe around a tick: *what produces the thing\nthe tick consumes?*  If the answer is `timer_add`, the probe must free-run, because `debugger.c`\ncalls `timer_stop()` while interacting and `step N` returns before `timer_start()`.\n\n    DEVICE_TICK(wdc)\n    {\n            struct wdc_data *d = (struct wdc_data *) extra;\n\n            if (d->int_assert)\n                    INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n    }\n\n**What it consumes is `d->int_assert`, a flag the COMMAND PATH sets from an ordinary guest register\nwrite.**  No host timer produces it.  And tick functions do run while stepping -- `machine.c`\ndecrements `ticks_till_next[te]` by 1 under `single_step` instead of by `N_SAFE_DYNTRANS_LIMIT` --\nso the tick fires, one tick at a time.\n\n> **PREDICTED ROUTE: a guest write issues a wdc command that sets `int_assert`; the probe steps far\n> enough for the tick to run; `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq)` dispatches to the registered handler for\n> `<machine>.lca`; and that handler is `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.**  Synchronous in the carrier's sense\n> -- what the tick consumes comes from the guest, not from SIGALRM.\n\n**STILL A PREDICTION, AND THREE LINKS ARE UNVERIFIED:** (1) that `INTERRUPT_ASSERT` on `d->irq`\nresolves to the LCA handler rather than to an intermediate ISA controller that swallows it; (2) how\nmany steps the tick needs; (3) whether `alphabook1` reaches a state where a wdc command can be\nissued from a cold debugger at all.\n\n**LINK (1) IS THE ONE TO READ FIRST.**  `bus_isa_init` may interpose its own interrupt controller\nbetween the IDE channel and the `<machine>.lca` path -- in which case the assert never reaches these\nstubs, and the row's severity drops sharply.  **If it interposes, THAT is the finding, not a\nfailure**, and the row should be re-graded rather than quietly dropped.\n\n*Recorded because it converts an open-ended witness question into three named, checkable links.*\n\n---\n\n*** A PROCESS NOTE, because it happened while writing THIS paragraph and it is the second instance\ntoday. ***  The first attempt to record the above went through `python -c '...'` and bash tore the\nsingle-quoted string apart on the apostrophes in \"guest's\" and \"carrier's\", exactly as it had done\nhours earlier on a different note.  **The rule minted from the first instance -- PROSE DESTINED FOR\nA RECORD GOES INTO A SCRIPT FILE, NEVER THROUGH `-c` -- was already written down in this same ledger\nwhen I broke it again.**  That is the carrier's own structure-versus-memory finding landing on me\ntwice in one session: knowing a rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems,\nand only the second is solved by writing more.  This paragraph was written from a file and landed\nintact on the first attempt.\n\n*** LINK (1) IS SETTLED BY READING, AND IT RAISES THE SEVERITY RATHER THAN LOWERING IT.  THE\nINTERPOSED PIC DOES NOT SWALLOW THE ASSERT -- IT FORWARDS IT, AND BOTH STUBS ARE REACHABLE. ***\n\nI flagged `bus_isa_init` as possibly interposing a controller that absorbs the interrupt.  **It does\ninterpose, and it forwards.**  The full chain, every link read:\n\n    dev_wdc.c    DEVICE_TICK(wdc):  if (d->int_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n                                    -- d->irq is \"<machine>.lca.isa.14\" (wdc0_irq = 14,\n                                       wdc1_irq = 15, bus_isa.c:180)\n\n    bus_isa.c    isa_interrupt_assert():  sets the PIC IRR bit for that line, then\n                                          isa_interrupt_common(d, old_isa_assert)\n\n    bus_isa.c    isa_interrupt_common():  cascades PIC2 -> PIC1 IRQ 2, then\n                                          new_isa_assert = d->pic1->irr & ~d->pic1->ier;\n                                          if (old == new) return;\n                                          if (!new) { INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq); return; }\n                                          ... otherwise asserts upward\n\n    dev_lca.c    lca_interrupt_assert() / lca_interrupt_deassert():\n                                          fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n\n**`d->irq` in `bus_isa_data` is the connection made at `bus_isa.c:193`:\n`INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path, d->irq)` -- and `DEVINIT(lca)` passes `interrupt_path` =\n`\"<machine>.lca\"` as that base path.  So the upward assert lands exactly on the two stubs.**\n\n**AND `BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC` IS NOT SET BY LCA**, so the whole block at `bus_isa.c:190-209` runs:\nLCA passes only `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`.  The 16 ISA lines are registered and the upward\nconnection is made.\n\n*** BOTH DIRECTIONS KILL, WHICH I HAD NOT EXPECTED. ***  `isa_interrupt_common` calls\n**`INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq)`** at the point where the last unmasked IRR bit clears -- and\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` is the same bare `fatal()` + `exit(1)`.  So an ordinary interrupt LIFECYCLE\n-- assert, then acknowledge -- reaches a terminating stub at BOTH ends.  A fix that de-fatalises\nonly the assert leaves the deassert live.\n\n**THE ONE REMAINING GATE, and it is NOT a guest-unmask requirement in the direction I first\nassumed:** the upward assert fires when `pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier` becomes non-zero, i.e. when an IRR\nbit is set and NOT masked by `ier`.  **`ier` is the PIC's interrupt-ENABLE/mask register, and its\nRESET VALUE has NOT been read here** (`dev_8259.c`).  If it starts at 0 -- nothing masked -- then\nthe FIRST IDE assert propagates immediately with no guest setup at all.  **That is the next thing to\nread, and it decides whether this is \"reachable after ordinary driver setup\" or \"reachable\nimmediately\".**  Recorded as unread rather than guessed.\n\n**SEVERITY STAYS HIGH AND IS NOT RAISED TO CRITICAL YET**, because reachability is still established\nby READING only -- no witness has been run.  What has changed is that the plausible-interposition\nescape hatch is now closed: the PIC forwards, and both stubs are on the path.\n\n*** THE LAST GATE IS OPEN AT RESET.  THE CHAIN IS NOW COMPLETE WITH EVERY LINK READ, AND IT NEEDS NO\nGUEST SETUP AT ALL. ***\n\nThe remaining question was the PIC mask: the upward assert fires when `pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier` goes\nnon-zero, so a non-zero reset `ier` would mask IDE and gate the whole path behind guest driver\nconfiguration.  Read:\n\n    dev_8259.c:266    memset(d, 0, sizeof(struct pic8259_data));\n    dev_8259.c:213    d->ier = idata;          <- the ONLY write to ier, from a guest store\n\n**`ier` is zeroed at init and is never set anywhere else.**  So at reset `ier == 0`, `irr & ~ier`\nis just `irr`, and **the FIRST set IRR bit propagates upward immediately.**  Nothing is masked until\na guest deliberately masks it.\n\n*** THE COMPLETE CHAIN, EVERY LINK READ IN THE SOURCE: ***\n\n    1.  dev_wdc.c      DEVICE_TICK(wdc): if (d->int_assert) INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);\n                       `int_assert` is set by the guest COMMAND path -- no host timer involved.\n                       d->irq is \"<machine>.lca.isa.14\"  (wdc0_irq = 14, wdc1_irq = 15)\n\n    2.  bus_isa.c      isa_interrupt_assert(): sets the PIC IRR bit, calls isa_interrupt_common()\n\n    3.  bus_isa.c      isa_interrupt_common(): new = pic1->irr & ~pic1->ier;  ier == 0 AT RESET,\n                       so `new` is non-zero on the first assert -> forwards upward on d->irq\n                       (and calls INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->irq) when the last unmasked bit clears)\n\n    4.  bus_isa.c:193  d->irq came from INTERRUPT_CONNECT(interrupt_base_path, d->irq), and\n                       DEVINIT(lca) passes interrupt_path = \"<machine>.lca\" as that base path\n\n    5.  dev_lca.c:70   lca_interrupt_assert():   fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n        dev_lca.c:82   lca_interrupt_deassert(): fatal(\"...TODO\"); exit(1);\n\n**So on `alphabook1`, an ordinary IDE interrupt ends the host process, and BOTH ends of the\ninterrupt lifecycle -- assert and deassert -- land on a terminating stub.**  No unusual register\nvalue, no undocumented offset, no firmware revision, and no guest PIC configuration: the mask is\nopen at reset.\n\n*** WHAT IS AND IS NOT CLAIMED, because this is the point at which overclaiming would be easy. ***\n* **ESTABLISHED BY READING, link by link:** the wiring, the forwarding, the open reset mask, and\n  that both stubs terminate unconditionally.\n* **NOT MEASURED.**  No probe has been run.  Nothing here shows that a guest on `alphabook1`\n  actually issues a wdc command that sets `int_assert`, nor that the tick runs before the guest\n  does something else.  **The row does NOT claim \"alphabook1 cannot boot\"** -- that is a plausible\n  consequence, not a finding, and it would need a witness.\n* The severity assessment therefore stays **HIGH on the wiring**.  It goes to the top of the\n  critical queue only when a witness lands, and the honest form of that sentence is what this row\n  says rather than what it implies.\n\n**A FIX MUST TAKE BOTH STUBS.**  De-fatalising only `lca_interrupt_assert` leaves\n`lca_interrupt_deassert` live on the same path, reached at acknowledge time -- which would convert\n\"dies on the first disk interrupt\" into \"dies on the first disk interrupt ACK\" and read as a fix in\nevery test that only asserts.\n\n*Found by following [[lcabadval]]'s own eleven-kill census, which had already named these two as\n\"plausibly the most severe of the eleven\" and which nothing had followed up.*\n\n*** CORRECTION TO THIS ROW'S OWN PRECEDENT CLAIM: [[lunaabsentcpu]] TRANSFERS AS A **WITNESS**\nTECHNIQUE AND **NOT** AS A DISPOSITION.  Checked by reading its diff rather than its title. ***\n\nThe row above says *\"read that round before designing this one\"*, which reads as though its FIX\ntransfers.  It does not.  `79cd12e`'s diff on `dev_luna88k.c` is a **BOUND** repair:\n\n    -   for (cpu = 0; cpu < MAX_CPUS; cpu++)\n    +   for (cpu = 0; cpu < d->ncpus; cpu++)          /*  #503  */\n\nplus a captured `ncpus` snapshot.  **luna's defect was walking into UNPOPULATED slots and calling a\ncallback that was the memset's NULL** -- a SIGSEGV from an absent handler.  **LCA's defect is a\nhandler that is PRESENT and deliberately terminates.**  Bounding a walk cannot help when the walk is\ncorrect and the destination is `exit(1)`; there is no unconnected slot here.\n\n**WHAT DOES TRANSFER, and it is the more useful half: HOW TO DRIVE AN INTERRUPT FROM GUEST STORES\nALONE.**  `luna_absentcpu_probe.py`'s witness is *\"a guest store of 0xfc000000 to INT_ST_MASK1\nfollowed by a store to SOFT_INT1\"* -- **unmask, then trigger, both ordinary guest stores, no host\ntimer and no disk.**  That is exactly the shape a witness for this row needs, and it is why the\n`#503` round is worth reading.\n\n**The ISA analogue is not identical**, because there is no soft-interrupt register on this bus: the\nPIC mask is already open at reset (so the unmask step is free) but the TRIGGER has to come from a\ndevice asserting, which is what the reachability census now out to codex is enumerating.\n\n*This is the warrant-transfer caution in miniature: a precedent that is genuinely same-family for\none purpose (witness construction) can be wrong for another (disposition), and the row title looks\nidentical either way.  Read the diff, not the title.*\n\n*** MEASURED 2026-09-07.  THE MOST SEVERE ITEM IN THIS FILE IS NOW A RUNG-3 WITNESS, AND IT TAKES\nFOUR GUEST INSTRUCTIONS. ***  `regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py`, artifact\n`regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/witness_HEAD_20260907.raw`, sentinel `LCAIRQ_DONE`.\n\n    lpt ctl      yes     yes     CONTROL: same store, same window, a non-asserting port   ok\n    kbc live     yes     yes     DEVICE SIGNATURE: dev_pckbc_access announced the command ok\n    kbc kill     dead    alive   <- THE DEFECT                                            FAIL\n    kbc attrib   yes     yes     ATTRIBUTION                                              ok\n      EVIDENCE: [ pckbc: write to CTL: aa 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 ]\n      EVIDENCE: lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n    LCAIRQ_RESULT=3/4\n\n**The whole program is four instructions** -- build the scratch pointer, load the port address, load\nthe value, store it -- on unmodified `alphabook1`, and `0xAA` is the STANDARD 8042 SELF-TEST\ncommand.  No disk image, no OS, no host timer, no driver setup, no unusual value.\n\n*** THE MATCHED CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES IT ATTRIBUTABLE, and it is the census that supplied it. ***\n`lpt ctl` performs the IDENTICAL store -- same value, same width, same K0SEG path, same ISA window\n-- one port away, at `B+0x378`, where `dev_lpt_tick` is EMPTY and `dev_lpt_access` never asserts.\nIt SURVIVES.  So the death is attributable to the DEVICE REACHED, not to storing into ISA space,\nnot to the address width, and not to the debugger path.\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE WERE CORRECTED BY THE codex ISA CENSUS BEFORE THIS WAS BUILT**, and the row\nwould have failed without both: the WDC controllers exist ONLY IF an IDE image is attached, so the\nIDE route this row was written around is unavailable to a probe attaching a memory file; and PIT/RTC\nasserts are HOST-TIMER driven and therefore invisible to a stepped probe, while PCKBC and the UARTs\nare synchronous with the guest access.  *The census cost one codex invocation and it chose the route.*\n\n*** SEVERITY: the wiring claim is now MEASURED, not read.  This is a guest-drivable host kill in four\ninstructions through a documented register. ***  The row's earlier caution -- \"severity stays HIGH on\nthe wiring; it goes to the top of the critical queue only when a witness lands\" -- is now discharged\non its own terms.\n\n**STILL NOT CLAIMED:** that any shipped guest issues an 8042 self-test at that moment.  The witness\nshows the path is open and ordinary, not that a particular OS walks it.\n\n**AND THE CONTRACT LIMIT IS THE SAME ONE grok NAMED ON THE SIBLING ROW:** this witnesses THIS\n`exit(1)`; it is NOT a detector of a repair.  \"Host survives\" would green for a silent return, for a\nlatched announce, and for an actual interrupt implementation alike.  **A fix round owes a row that\ndiscriminates those** -- and note the deassert stub is on the same path and would need its own.\n\n*** TREE-WIDE CENSUS: THIS IS THE ONLY TERMINATING INTERRUPT HANDLER IN THE FORK, 2 OF 70.  The\nsibling grep is discharged with a denominator, and it comes back BOUNDED. ***\n\n    distinct functions ASSIGNED as an interrupt handler   70\n    of those, bodies located                              70      (no blind spots)\n    whose body calls exit()                                2      BOTH in dev_lca.c\n        lca_interrupt_assert      dev_lca.c:70\n        lca_interrupt_deassert    dev_lca.c:82\n    registered handlers that do NOT terminate             68\n\n**So this is NOT a class -- it is the tree's single outlier**, and no sibling hunt is owed.\n\n*** AND THE CENSUS WAS RUN TWICE ON PURPOSE, KEYED TWO DIFFERENT WAYS, BECAUSE THE FIRST KEY WAS A\nCORRELATE. ***  Pass 1 matched functions whose NAME ends in `interrupt_assert`/`_deassert` and found\n**69**.  Pass 2 keyed on what actually DEFINES the class -- a function ASSIGNED to\n`.interrupt_assert` / `.interrupt_deassert` -- and found **70**.  **One registered handler does not\nfollow the naming convention and was invisible to the name-keyed pass.**  Both agree on the answer\n(2), but only the second could have been trusted to give it: this project's deriver rule is to key\non what makes a member a member, and a name is a correlate whose error is invisible because the\noutput is still a plausible number.\n\n*** THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE FIX IS THE USEFUL HALF: THERE ARE 68 WORKING PRECEDENTS. ***  The codex\nfix brief asks (Q2) whether an in-tree shape can be copied.  The census answers it before the seat\ndoes -- 68 registered handlers deliver, latch, forward or ignore an interrupt without ending the\nprocess, so the question is not *whether* a non-terminating handler is possible on this\narchitecture but *which* of the 68 shapes suits an Alpha ISA line.\n\n**AND IT STRENGTHENS THE CASE THAT THESE TWO ARE UPSTREAM RESIDUE RATHER THAN A DECISION** -- the\nsame reading the option-set rule reached for `sgireunmapped`, where the sibling handlers in the same\nfile already declined and the arm under review was the file's own outlier.  Here the outlier is\n2-of-70 across the whole fork.\n\n*** THE codex FIX DESIGN LANDED AND IT SOLVES THE PROBLEM BOTH WITNESS CONTRACTS DECLARED OPEN.\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/codex_fix_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**DENOMINATOR: 36/36 registration call sites in 28 files, 35 assert/deassert pairs = 70 callbacks.\nThat INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCES this row's own census of 70**, by a different method (registration\nsites rather than assignment targets).  Two derivations, same number.\n\n*** 1.  MEMBER 7 (IMPLEMENT) IS BLOCKED AT THE DYNTRANS CORE, NOT AT THE DEVICE. ***\n`alpha_irq_interrupt_assert()` exists and sets `cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 1`.  **But \"the only\nreferences to Alpha's `irq_asserted` are the two assignments\"** -- nothing consumes it -- and\n`DYNTRANS_RUN_INSTR_DEF`'s interrupt-dispatch block has **ARM, M88K, MIPS, PPC and SH branches and\nNO ALPHA BRANCH**.  So even correct LCA wiring would deliver nothing to guest software.  **This\nindependently confirms the filed row [[irqassertedunread]]** -- \"alpha, i960 and riscv set\ncd.<arch>.irq_asserted with no consumer\" -- reached from the other direction.\n\nA minimal wiring step is still identified and is NOT sufficient on its own: give `lca_data` a\nconnected upstream `struct interrupt`, connect it to `devinit->interrupt_path` (which already\ndefaults to the bootstrap CPU in `device_add()`), and forward both levels.  **LCA currently stores\nno upstream interrupt connection at all**, and passing its own object to the CPU callback would be\nwrong because `extra` points at `lca_data` rather than the destination CPU.\n\n*** 2.  MY Q3 FRAMING WAS REFUTED: THERE IS NO HOUSE POLICY FOR THIS CASE. ***  I asked which\ndisposition \"this tree's own practice favours\".  The answer is that the precedents CONFLICT:\n\n    cpu_sh.c    sh3_cpu_interrupt_assert/deassert()   silent TODO bodies\n    dev_pcc2.c  reassert_interrupts()                 diagnoses, then exit(1)\n    dev_sh4.c   sh4_timer_tick()                      latched announce, then continues\n\n*\"The SH4 example supports bounded reporting, but it does not establish a universal disposition for\nan asserted delivery path.\"*  My brief presumed a settled practice; there is none.\n\n*** 3.  A DISPOSITION I HAD NOT NAMED, WITH AN IN-TREE PRECEDENT: DIAGNOSE AND STOP THE GUEST CPU. ***\nNot `exit(1)` (host process death) and not a silent or latched return (invisible loss), but an\nEMULATOR-LIMITATION STOP: `alpha_palcode()` already does exactly this for unsupported PAL calls --\ndiagnostic plus `cpu->running = 0`.  **This is an emulator limitation, not an architectural guest\nFAULT**, and the distinction is the point.\n\n**AND A CAUTION THAT WOULD HAVE MADE A NAIVE FIX SUBTLY WRONG:** *\"merely changing `running` inside a\ndevice callback does not itself unwind the current batched execution.\"*  `alpha_instr_call_pal()`\nhandles PAL stops by redirecting `next_ic` to `nothing_call`; a device implementation must arrange\nthe same unwind.  *That is the same class as the four-readers-proposed-`return` incident -- a\nplausible edit that skips machinery the enclosing control flow depends on.*\n\n*** 4.  THE DISCRIMINATING ROW, WHICH IS WHAT BOTH WITNESS CONTRACTS SAID WAS MISSING. ***\n**\"Stopped guest state, plus the reason -- NOT survival.\"**  Proposed acceptance row: starting from\na running CPU, an unsupported LCA assertion produces its specific diagnostic and leaves\n`cpu.running == false`; with debugger-at-end enabled the debugger stays responsive and subsequent\nsteps execute no guest instructions; the matched non-asserting control remains RUNNING.\n\n    diagnosed guest stop                              GREEN\n    silent return                                     RED -- CPU remains running\n    latched announce and return                       RED -- CPU remains running\n    forwarding only, or delivery to a resuming handler RED -- CPU remains running\n    the existing exit(1)                              RED -- debugger/process is gone\n\n**That discriminates all five candidates**, and the state is already observable: `cpu_new()` exposes\n`running` as a setting, `machine_run()` skips stopped CPUs, and `emul_run()` enters the debugger\nafter all machines stop when debugger-at-end is set.\n\n> **SO THE CONTRACT LIMIT ON BOTH LCA WITNESSES IS LIFTED -- for THIS disposition.**  Each says\n> \"host survives\" cannot tell the fix members apart, and that remains true; the answer is that the\n> oracle must read the CPU's RUNNING STATE and the diagnostic, not the host's survival.  The\n> existing probes would need that row added; neither has it today.\n\n**THE FABLE QUESTION IN §7j-1 IS NARROWED BUT NOT ANSWERED.**  I asked whether a latched decline is\nacceptable for an unimplemented delivery path.  codex's answer is that a THIRD option dominates both\nhorns -- but choosing between \"stop the guest with a diagnostic\" and \"lose interrupts quietly and\nkeep running\" is still a judgment about what an emulator owes its user, and that is exactly what the\nflagship seat is held for.  **§7j-2 (what observable discriminates) is now ANSWERED and can be\nstruck from the queue when the adjudication runs.**\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING CLAIM WAS VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, AND MY FIRST CHECK NEARLY REFUTED IT\nWRONGLY. ***\n\ncodex's whole disposition rests on \"the Alpha CPU cannot take an asynchronous interrupt\".  Checked:\n\n    grep -rn 'alpha.irq_asserted' src/     ->  2 hits, BOTH assignments in cpu_alpha.c\n    the dyntrans interrupt dispatch        ->  cpu_dyntrans.c tests cd.arm.irq_asserted (:208),\n                                               cd.m88k.irq_asserted (:212, :382),\n                                               cd.ppc.irq_asserted (:243) -- and NO cd.alpha.\n\n**CONFIRMED.**  Nothing consumes the Alpha flag and no dispatch branch exists for it.\n\n**MY FIRST CHECK WAS A CORRELATE AND IT FOUND A FALSE POSITIVE.**  I grepped\n`#if defined(DYNTRANS_ALPHA)` in `cpu_dyntrans.c` and got a hit at `:486` -- which is\n`n_args_to_print` in a DISASSEMBLY function and has nothing to do with interrupts.  Read as a\nrefutation, that hit would have produced a confident wrong correction to a seat that was right.\n**The fix was to search for the THING (an `irq_asserted` test) rather than for a correlate (the\narchitecture ifdef)** -- the deriver rule, on a one-line verification.\n\n*** A FACT THIS ROW'S QUESTION DOES NOT CARRY, ESTABLISHED 2026-09-08 FROM THREE INDEPENDENT FILES:\nOPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS NOT AVAILABLE ON ALPHA. ***\n\nThis is an Alpha machine, and the adjudication of this row weighs dispositions from the standing\noption set.  **FAULT looks available and is not**, which is the option-set-completeness problem\ninverted -- not a missing member, a PHANTOM one.\n\n    memory_rw.c        the device-failure dispatcher has MIPS and M88K exception arms and\n                       NO ALPHA ARM                          (found via dev_lca.c, `lcaisalane`)\n    memory_alpha.c     no MM-fault dispatch exists at all    (found via `alphapteprot`)\n    cpu_alpha_palcode  ALPHA_KENTRY_MM and ALPHA_MMCSR_* are DEFINED and have NO CONSUMERS;\n                       kentry[] is written by wrent and initialised to a dummy PAL, never\n                       dispatched                            (found via `alphatbinegsel` Q3)\n\n**Three briefs reached this from three directions, none of them asked about it.**  And a fourth\nseat priced it: choosing FAULT obliges building delivery first -- a PAL frame,\n`kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a redirected PC.  *\"That is\na new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.\"*\n\n> **SO ANY ANSWER TO THIS ROW THAT REACHES FOR FAULT IS REACHING FOR A ROUND THAT DOES NOT EXIST\n> YET.**  It remains a legitimate ANSWER -- but it is an answer with a prerequisite, and the\n> prerequisite is large.  Recorded here so the adjudication prices it rather than assuming it.\n\n*** THE NOTE ABOVE UNDERSTATED THE SCOPE, AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART: FAULT IS NOT\n\"UNAVAILABLE ON ALPHA\".  IT IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**, FOR EVERY ARCHITECTURE IN THIS\nTREE.  Derived 2026-09-08 by opening the dispatcher. ***\n\n`src/cpus/memory_rw.c`, the device-failure path -- the whole of it:\n\n    if (res <= 0 && !no_exceptions) {\n            debug(\"[ %s device '%s' addr %08lx failed ]\");\n    #ifdef MEM_MIPS\n            mips_cpu_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION? EXCEPTION_IBE : EXCEPTION_DBE, ...);\n    #endif\n    #ifdef MEM_M88K\n            m88k_exception(cpu, cache == CACHE_INSTRUCTION ? M88K_EXCEPTION_INSTRUCTION_ACCESS\n                                                           : M88K_EXCEPTION_DATA_ACCESS, 0);\n    #endif\n            return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED;\n    }\n\n**TWO `#ifdef` ARMS.  There is no ARM arm, no Alpha arm, no PPC arm and no SH arm** -- every other\narchitecture reaches `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered at all.\n\n> **SO OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 IS A PHANTOM ON EVERY ARCHITECTURE BUT TWO**, and a round on an ARM, SH,\n> PPC or Alpha device that reaches for FAULT is reaching for a round that does not exist yet.\n\n*Found because I wrote the OPPOSITE into a brief.  Assessing `fbfiqsilent` -- an ARM footbridge row\n-- I told the seat that \"member 5 FAULT may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw`'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm.\"  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.*\n\n*** codex: CONFIRMED GUEST-REACHABLE PROCESS TERMINATION ON alphabook1, WITH THE PATH NAMED. ***\n\n    dev_ns16550_access() / scheduled UART tick -> dev_ns16550_tick()\n        -> isa_interrupt_assert() -> isa_interrupt_common() -> lca_interrupt_assert() -> exit(1)\n\n**The decisive wiring is one line after the registration:** `devinit_lca()` passes the same\ninterrupt path to `bus_isa_init()` (`dev_lca.c:418`), which connects its upstream interrupt to the\nsupplied template (`bus_isa.c:190`) and **unconditionally installs the first UART on ISA IRQ 4**\n(`bus_isa.c:250` -- verified by reading: the `ns16550 irq=%s.isa.%i` device_add with `4`).  No\nmachine-description modification is involved, which is what makes it a witness candidate rather\nthan laundering.\n\n**LCA line 0 is the AGGREGATE ISA input, not ISA IRQ 0** -- a correction to my brief's reading of\nthe comment.  And missing Alpha CPU dispatch does not prevent reaching the callback: *the process\nexits before CPU acceptance matters*.\n\n*** THE REPAIR IS EXPLICITLY PARTIAL, AND I VERIFIED THE REASON MYSELF. ***  codex says forwarding\nto an upstream `struct interrupt` is the minimum useful repair but cannot complete delivery,\nbecause the Alpha CPU callbacks only set and clear `cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted` and **that field\nhas no reader**.  Checked by census:\n\n    cd.alpha.irq_asserted   written cpu_alpha.c:218, :223   declared cpu_alpha.h:161   READ NOWHERE\n    cd.arm.irq_asserted     READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:208\n    cd.m88k.irq_asserted    READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:212 and :382\n    cd.ppc.irq_asserted     READ at cpu_dyntrans.c:243 and cpu_ppc.c:309\n\n**`cpu_dyntrans.c` has no Alpha arm at all.**  So a correct forwarding fix would set a flag nothing\nconsumes -- honest containment, not delivery.\n\n**FOOTBRIDGE IS THE CLOSEST IN-TREE ANALOGUE and it does NOT transfer literally:** it registers\n`interrupt->line` as a **bit mask** while LCA registers **index zero**, so a copied\n`status |= interrupt->line` would record nothing.\n\n*** MY OWN CITATIONS WERE CONTENTS ENTRIES, WHICH codex CAUGHT. ***  The brief cited manual lines\n`175` and `510` for the interrupt sections; those are TABLE-OF-CONTENTS rows.  The bodies are at\n`:4459` (S4.1.13) and `:10729` (S6.4.13).  *I had read the bodies and then cited the contents.*\n\n*** WITNESS ATTEMPT -- MADE, HARNESS WORKS, AND IT DID **NOT** FIRE.  Recorded as an attempt,\nwhich is what the lifecycle rule asks for. ***\n\n`_scratchpad/lcairq_probe.py`, harness copied from `regress/lca_confbus_probe.py` (pty.fork,\n`-V -x -E alpha -e alphabook1 -M 64 0x20000000:alpha_dummy.bin`).  Addresses derived, not guessed:\n`LCA_ISA_BASE = LCA_PCI_SIO(0x1c0000000) + 0x10000000`, UART at `+0x3f8`, K0SEG for the debugger.\n\n    live        alive   alive   CONTROL: plain step on an unmodified machine          ok\n    devsig      alive   alive   CONTROL: the UART window reads through the same decode ok\n    spin only   alive   alive   CONTROL: free-running self-branch, no UART setup       ok\n    irq assert  alive   died    IER=0x02, MCR=0x08, THR write, then `continue`    *** did not fire ***\n\n**Three controls green and the writes visibly land** (the debugger echoes each `put b`).  Two\nthings were learned and are worth carrying: the 8-byte all-zero dummy decodes as `call_pal\nPAL_halt` and **stops the machine before any device tick can run**, which is why the first attempt\nneeded a planted self-branch (`br r31,-1` = `0xc3ffffff`); and even free-running with IER, MCR and\na THR write in place, no assertion reached the stub.\n\n**THE OPEN QUESTION IS WHETHER A DEBUGGER `put` REACHES `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)` AT ALL.**\n`dev_ns16550.c:353` registers via `memory_device_register`, and this project already records that\ndebugger access can bypass a handler depending on the registration flags.  If `put` writes a shadow\nrather than the device, `IIR_TXRDY` was never set and the tick's condition was never true -- which\nwould make the attempt inconclusive rather than negative.  **That is the next thing to settle, and\nit is a measurement, so it belongs to the reserved measuring seat rather than to another reading.**\n\n*So the row stands on codex's SOURCE-DERIVED reachability, not on an executed witness.  The\ndistinction matters and is recorded rather than blurred.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS ATTEMPT'S OPEN QUESTION IS NOW SETTLED, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT MY APPARATUS COULD\nNOT DO WHAT I ASKED OF IT.  A DEBUGGER `put' NEVER REACHES `DEVICE_ACCESS(ns16550)'. ***\n\n    debugger_cmds.c:1461, :1507   memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE, CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS)\n    memory_rw.c:584               if (!no_exceptions || (flags & DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS))\n                                          -> only THEN is devices[i].f() called\n    dev_ns16550.c:353-355         memory_device_register(..., DM_DEFAULT, NULL)\n\n`DM_DEFAULT` is 0, so the UART carries no `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS` bit; the debugger passes\n`NO_EXCEPTIONS`; **the handler is skipped.**  My three `put b' writes to IER, MCR and THR were\nabsorbed without ever invoking `dev_ns16550_access`, so `IIR_TXRDY` was never set, the tick's\ncondition was never true, and nothing asserted.\n\n> **SO THE ATTEMPT WAS INCONCLUSIVE FOR AN APPARATUS REASON, AND THAT IS NOW A FACT RATHER THAN A\n> SUSPICION.**  It says nothing whatever about whether the defect is reachable -- codex's\n> source-derived path stands untouched.\n\n*** AND IT GENERALISES INTO A PROBE-WRITING RULE THIS PROJECT HAD ONLY HALF-RECORDED. ***  The\ncarrier already notes that the debugger's `dump` bypasses a `DM_DEFAULT` handler on READS, and\nprescribes guest loads for SH4 state.  **The same gate governs WRITES, by the same line of\n`memory_rw.c`** -- so:\n\n> **CONFIGURING A DEVICE FROM THE DEBUGGER WITH `put' IS A NO-OP AGAINST ANY `DM_DEFAULT' DEVICE.\n> Device access in a probe must be made by GUEST INSTRUCTIONS.**  `put' is for RAM -- scratch\n> pointers, planted code, page tables -- where no handler is involved.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PROBES ALREADY DO THIS CORRECTLY, which is why nothing else is broken:**\n`lca_confbus_probe.py` uses `put d' only to seed SCRATCH in RAM and then reaches the device with\nguest `STQ`/`LDQ`; `alpha_swpctx_v0_probe.py` (#514) uses `put' only for RAM and `call_pal', and\ntouches no device at all.  **My failed attempt is the one that broke the pattern**, and it broke it\nby configuring a device the debugger cannot configure.\n\n**WHAT THE WITNESS NOW NEEDS, concretely:** the UART's IER/MCR/THR must be written by guest stores.\nThe registers are byte-wide at `LCA_ISA_BASE + 0x3f8` with `addrmult = 1`, so this needs either a\nbyte store (`STB`, which is a BWX instruction and may not exist on a 21064-class part -- CHECK\nbefore assuming) or a wider store whose other bytes are harmless.  **That check is the next step\nand it is cheap; it is recorded here rather than guessed.**\n\n*** THE WITNESS FIRES.  `regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py', measured on `2ab3d2f' with `src/'\nUNMODIFIED: 2/4, and the transcript's last line is the stub's own diagnostic. ***\n\n    live         alive   alive   CONTROL: a free-running self-branch alone survives        ok\n    ram ctl      alive   alive   ATTRIBUTION: the IDENTICAL sequence against RAM survives   ok\n    uart irq     died    alive   an ordinary UART interrupt must not end the emulator     FAIL\n    stub named   yes     no      the stub's own TODO diagnostic must not appear           FAIL\n\n    GXemul> continue\n    lca_interrupt_assert: TODO\n\n**THREE GUEST BYTE STORES END THE EMULATOR** -- IER `0x02`, MCR `0x08`, then any byte to THR --\non an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1` with no `device_add` and no machine-description change.\n`WITNESS_HEAD_20260908.out` and the full session transcript are preserved beside it.\n\n*** `ram ctl' IS WHAT MAKES THIS A WITNESS RATHER THAN A CRASH REPORT. ***  It runs the\n**identical instruction sequence** -- same registers, same opcodes, same count -- against RAM\ninstead of the UART, and survives.  So the termination is attributable to the DEVICE and not to the\ncode sequence, the self-branch, or the free-running mode.  One control, two hypotheses separated.\n\n*** IT TOOK THREE FACTS TO GET HERE, AND TWO OF THEM WERE MINE TO GET WRONG. ***\n\n1. **codex's source path**, which named the wiring end to end: `devinit_lca()` hands its interrupt\n   path to `bus_isa_init()`, which unconditionally installs the first ns16550 on ISA IRQ 4.\n2. **The `put'/DM_DEFAULT finding** -- my FIRST attempt configured the UART with debugger `put' and\n   nothing happened, because `put' passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS' and `memory_rw' then skips a `DM_DEFAULT'\n   device's handler entirely.  *That attempt was inconclusive for an apparatus reason, and until it\n   was traced it looked like evidence the path was unreachable.*\n3. **`stb' is ungated in this tree.**  The UART registers are byte-wide, so the guest needs a byte\n   store -- a BWX instruction a real 21066 would not have.  `cpu_alpha_instr.c:895` decodes\n   `case 0x0e` with **no feature check**, and `ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX` (`cpu_alpha.h:57`) is **DEFINED\n   AND NEVER CONSULTED** -- the third declared-but-unused constant found in this header today,\n   after `ALPHA_PTE_ASM` and `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM`.  **So this probe depends on an emulator fact rather\n   than a hardware one, and a future round that gates BWX would break it.**  Recorded in the probe\n   header, not left to be rediscovered.\n\n**SEVERITY: the row is already `high` and this does not raise it -- it removes the last doubt about\nreachability.**  The witness ladder is explicit that rung measures reachability-proof strength and\nnever harm, so a witness landing does not by itself move severity.  *What it does change is that\n\"guest-reachable\" is no longer source-derived.*\n\n**WHAT THE ROW STILL OWES is unchanged and is the hard part:** the disposition.  Forwarding cannot\ncomplete delivery, because `cd.alpha.irq_asserted` has no reader and `cpu_dyntrans.c` has no Alpha\narm -- so any repair converts *\"the emulator terminates\"* into *\"the interrupt is silently\ndropped\"*.  That question is with codex and is queued for the flagship seat.\n\n*** CORRECTION, AND IT IS MINE: THIS ROW WAS ALREADY WITNESSED AND I BUILT A SECOND WITNESS\nWITHOUT CHECKING.  I ALSO OVERWROTE THE COMMITTED PROBE FILE DOING IT. ***\n\n`regress/lca_irqstub_probe.py` shipped in **`7c2f8e4` on 2026-09-07** -- *\"lcairqstubkill\nWITNESSED: four guest instructions end the host through a documented 8042 register\"* -- scoring\n**3/4**, with an LPT control chosen from a codex ISA census because `dev_lpt_tick` is empty and\ncannot assert.  **The ledger's own `witness` field said all of that**, and I replaced it with a\nthinner sentence about my own probe.\n\n**HOW IT WAS CAUGHT: the probe-wiring checker's DUPLICATE-KEY guard.**  A dict literal keeps only\nthe last value and says nothing, so the checker refuses two entries for one key -- and it named\n`lca_irqstub_probe.py`.  Without that guard I would have shipped a manifest that silently\ndescribed the wrong probe.  *Nothing else in the gate would have noticed, because the file existed\nand the row had a witness either way.*\n\n**DAMAGE, checked rather than assumed:** the probe file was overwritten and is **restored from\nHEAD**; the ledger `witness` field is **restored and extended**.  **Yesterday's artifacts are\nintact** -- `witness_HEAD_20260907.raw` is unmodified, and `git diff HEAD` over that directory\nshows only my new files added.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES, AND IT IS WORTH KEEPING:** my probe is a **SECOND, INDEPENDENT PATH** -- ns16550\non IRQ 4 rather than 8042 on IRQ 1, three byte stores rather than one word store, with its own\nidentical-sequence-against-RAM control.  **Two independent guest routes to one stub is real\nvalue**: a repair that silences one and not the other is visible at once.  It is renamed\n`lca_irqstub_uart_probe.py` and its artifacts `UART_WITNESS_*`, so nothing can be mistaken for the\nrow's witness of record.\n\n**AND TWO FINDINGS FROM BUILDING IT STAND ON THEIR OWN**, independent of the duplication: the\n`put`/`DM_DEFAULT` mechanism (a debugger `put` never reaches a `DM_DEFAULT` device's handler, now\nfiled as a probe-writing hazard), and that `ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX` is defined and never consulted so\n`stb` executes on any Alpha here.\n\n> **THE PROCESS FAILURE IS EXACT AND WORTH NAMING: I read the row's `state_note` and its briefs,\n> and never read its `witness` FIELD.**  The standing instruction is to check the repo before\n> starting, and I checked the prose and not the schema.  *A row that already carries a witness is\n> the cheapest possible thing to check, and it is one field.*\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION IS DESIGNED, WITH TWO TRANSFERRED WARRANTS AND AN EXPLICIT LIMIT. ***\n\n**VERDICT (codex):** *bounded announcement plus level forwarding, as a PARTIAL repair, with Alpha\ninterrupt delivery left explicitly unresolved.*\n\n*** IT ARGUED THE STRONGEST CASE AGAINST THAT BEFORE HOLDING IT, which is what was asked. ***\n*\"It preserves the guest's failure while weakening its visibility.  An interrupt-dependent guest\ncan still hang; a warning can disappear into logs; an automation wrapper can mistake continued\nprocess life for success.  Forwarding adds no presently functioning guest capability because\nnothing consumes the flag.  An announced drop would achieve the same immediate containment with\nless plumbing.\"*\n\n> **AND THEN THE DISCRIMINATION THAT SETTLES IT:** *\"That objection defeats a claim of FIXED ALPHA\n> INTERRUPTS.  It does not defeat the narrower improvement of removing these two process exits.\"*\n> The round may remove the terminations; it may not claim delivery.\n\n**THE ADDITION LICENCE IS SATISFIED BY TWO NAMED IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, not by \"it cannot hurt\":**\nFootbridge's connection-and-propagation shape (`dev_footbridge.c:794`), and -- the one that matters\nfor the announce -- **`dev_pcc2.c:369`, which ALREADY ANNOUNCES AN UNAVAILABLE INTERRUPT-DELIVERY\nMODE ONCE.**  That is this exact disposition, already shipped elsewhere in the tree.\n\n**THE MINIMAL IMPLEMENTATION, concretely:** add `struct interrupt irq;` and `int\nirq_delivery_warned;` to `lca_data` (the existing `memset` initialises the latch);\n`INTERRUPT_CONNECT(devinit->interrupt_path, d->irq)` before creating downstream devices, since that\npath already defaults to the bootstrap CPU; a `lca_interrupt_notice()` helper carrying the `(once)`\nmarker; then `notice(); INTERRUPT_ASSERT(d->irq);` and the deassert mirror.  **N = 1 per instance\nwith an explicit suppression marker -- the house idiom, not the counter form.**\n\n*** IT ALSO CORRECTED ITS OWN EARLIER FOUR-ROW COVERAGE TABLE as having \"overstated what could\nimmediately become passing coverage\", which is a seat being harder on itself than on me: ***\n\n    callback forwarding      BUILDABLE TODAY as a host-side test; the current callbacks fail it\n                             and the small repair passes it\n    masking and release      SPLIT.  Level retention/withdrawal passes after the small repair;\n                             masked-versus-unmasked ENTRY requires CPU delivery, and no entry in\n                             either case proves nothing about masking\n    vector acknowledgement   BLOCKED on missing LCA acknowledgement support -- a SEPARATE missing\n                             dependency, implementable independently of CPU dispatch\n    guest execution          CANNOT pass with forwarding alone\n\n**SO THE ROUND'S DETECTOR IS THE FIRST ROW PLUS HALF THE SECOND**, and the record must say that\nrather than implying the table.\n\n*** AND THE DELIVERY ROUND IS BIGGER THAN A CONDITIONAL IN `cpu_dyntrans.c'. ***  codex found two\nmore declared-but-unused things in this layer: **the tree WRITES `kentry[]` AND NEVER CONSUMES\nIT**, and **`PAL_OSF1_rti` has a printable name with no execution implementation** -- so the PAL\ndispatcher cannot supply the interrupt entry/return lifecycle at all.  *That is the fifth\ndeclared-but-unused Alpha item found today, after `ALPHA_PTE_ASM`, `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and\n`ALPHA_FEATURE_BWX`.*  Queued as its own compatibility round with a named measured delta: on the\npartial-repair baseline, an eligible asserted input leaves a guest handler counter unchanged, and\nthe implementation makes the handler execute and return.\n\n**Footbridge supplies no Alpha PAL entry convention**, so that warrant does NOT transfer to the\ndelivery round -- only to the propagation shape.\n\n*** THE SIBLING CHECK THE ERROR DEMANDED, AND IT CAME BACK CLEAN. ***  Having overwritten one\ncommitted witness, the obvious question is whether I have done it anywhere else, or whether any\nother row's `witness' field names something that is no longer there.  Censused across every OPEN\nrow:\n\n    open rows                                        194\n    of which ALREADY CARRY A WITNESS                 119\n    whose witness names a MISSING file                 0\n\n**Zero.**  Every path named in a witness field exists on disk, so this was a single incident and\nthe restore closed it.  *And all EIGHT high-severity open rows already carry a witness* -- which\nsays something about the queue that is worth writing down: **the top of this queue is not short of\nevidence, it is short of DISPOSITIONS.**  That is the shape of work the flagship adjudication is\nfor, and it is why the packet's questions are disposition questions rather than requests for more\nmeasurement.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION QUESTION IS SETTLED, 2026-09-08, AND THE ANSWER IS THAT FORWARDING IS A NO-OP ON\nTHIS ARCHITECTURE.  Led by the codex seat, VERIFIED BY ME IN ONE GREP. ***\n\nThe round had been parked on *\"forwarding alone leaves Alpha delivery unresolved\"* without anyone\nestablishing what Alpha delivery actually IS.  A codex ANALYSIS job (see `codexcyberflag` -- the\nconstruction-shaped brief is refused, the analysis-shaped one is not) said mid-stream:\n\n> *\"Alpha has a CPU interrupt pin and an `irq_asserted` field, but the searches so far show only\n> WRITES to that field.\"*\n\n**Its stream then ended without a final verdict, so this would have been an UNREAD REVIEW had the\n`.err` not been opened.  Checked rather than taken:**\n\n    grep -rn irq_asserted src/          ->  57 references, classified\n\n    cpu_alpha.c:218    cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 1;      WRITE\n    cpu_alpha.c:223    cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted = 0;      WRITE\n    cpu_alpha.h:161    int irq_asserted;                    DECL\n    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    READS OF cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted ANYWHERE IN src/ :   ZERO\n\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` is where a pending interrupt becomes a delivered one, and it tests exactly three\narchitectures -- `cd.arm.irq_asserted` (`:208`), `cd.m88k.irq_asserted` (`:212`, `:382`) and\n`cd.ppc.irq_asserted` (`:243`).  **Alpha is absent from that list.**  `cpu_ppc.c:309` shows the\nsame field being consumed on the PPC side; there is no Alpha counterpart.\n\n> **SO THE ALPHA CPU MODEL HAS AN INTERRUPT-PENDING FLAG THAT NOTHING CONSUMES.  Wiring the LCA\n> stubs to `cpu_interrupt()` would set a field no code reads: a NO-OP, not a delivery.**\n\n*** WHAT THIS SETTLES, and it changes the round from blocked to shippable: ***\n\n* **Member 7 (IMPLEMENT) is NOT a wiring job.**  It requires adding Alpha interrupt dispatch to\n  `cpu_dyntrans.c` plus the PALcode entry and IPL check that go with it -- a SUBSYSTEM, and its own\n  round.  The earlier note's instinct that *\"declining an interrupt leaves the machine incomplete\"*\n  was right about the machine and wrong about the size of the gap: the machine is already\n  incomplete, before this row's stubs are reached.\n* **The partial repair is COHERENT, which was the open question.**  Nothing in the model blocks\n  waiting for an acknowledgement that cannot come, because nothing is waiting at all -- there is no\n  consumer.  Replacing `exit(1)` with a bounded announce leaves the guest with interrupts silently\n  dropped, which is EXACTLY what it already gets on every Alpha path that does not terminate.\n* **The honest disclosure the round owes** is therefore not *\"we chose not to forward\"* but\n  *\"Alpha interrupt delivery does not exist in this CPU model; these stubs cannot forward to\n  anything, and the fix removes a host termination without pretending to add delivery.\"*\n\n**AND IT REFRAMES THE SEVERITY HONESTLY IN BOTH DIRECTIONS.**  The termination is still\nguest-reachable and still witnessed twice (8042 command port, 3/4; ns16550 IRQ 4, 2/4), so the row\nstays HIGH.  But the fix buys *the host stops dying*, NOT *interrupts start working* -- and a round\nblock claiming the latter would be a wrong record.\n\n*Provenance worth keeping: this is the reading-seat-buys-the-class pattern exactly.  codex named the\nfamily (`only writes to that field`) and never finished its answer; one grep turned that into a\nmeasured fact that settles a design question two panels had left open.  The cost of NOT opening a\nzero-byte job's `.err` would have been another round of design argument.*\n\n*** PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  P4 SURVIVED A SEAT THAT TRIED TO REFUTE IT, AND FOUR OF MY BRIEF'S OTHER\nCLAIMS DID NOT. ***\n\n**grok: \"RATIFY member 3 (per-instance, two bits), do not forward, do not call this delivery.\nP4 holds and is STRONGER than the brief states.\"**  On Q1 specifically: *\"Yes. I tried to refute it\nand failed.  Delivery does not exist.\"*  **deepseek** conditionally accepted and did the correct\npacket-fed thing -- refused to ratify P4 as unverifiable from the packet and named the exact search\nthat settles it, rather than agreeing.\n\n*** THE FOUR CORRECTIONS, three of which change the round. ***\n\n**(1) Q2 WAS MIS-FRAMED AND THE QUESTION CANNOT BE ASKED.**  I asked whether the stubs should write\n`irq_asserted` anyway.  grok: *\"`interrupt->extra` is `struct lca_data *`, NOT a `struct cpu *`.\nThere is no `irq_asserted` in this file to write.  Forwarding is `INTERRUPT_CONNECT(cpu->path, ...)`\nplus `INTERRUPT_ASSERT`, i.e. CHIPSET WIRING, not a field store.\"*  So the option I was weighing does\nnot exist at these call sites at all -- **the answer is not \"no, don't\", it is \"there is nothing\nthere to write.\"**\n\n**(2) \"A DRIVER WILL USUALLY REACH BOTH WITHIN ONE INTERRUPT CYCLE\" IS FALSE OF THIS CODE, and I\nused it to argue for two latch bits.**  grok traced it: *\"`isa_interrupt_common` takes ONE EDGE per\ncall -- ASSERT if IRR went 0->nonzero, DEASSERT if it went nonzero->0, return if the aggregate\nstayed asserted.  The 8042 probe never reads the `0x55` reply, so it never deasserts.  The UART probe\nleaves IER on and free-runs, so tick keeps asserting.  **Existing witnesses do not touch\n`_deassert`.**\"*\n> **THE CONSEQUENCE IS COVERAGE DEBT AND IT IS DISCLOSED: the deassert latch is UNWITNESSED.**  Two\n> bits remain the right choice -- for the opposite reason to the one I gave -- because the two\n> callbacks are reached on DIFFERENT edges and a shared bit would report whichever arrived first.\n\n**(3) `detector:` AS THE WITNESS TERM IS A CATEGORY ERROR.**  I proposed it in Q5.  grok: *\"That term\nis for a fix with a measured detector and NO guest-path witness.  This row already has TWO rung-3\nprobes.  Unobservable DELIVERY is a CONTRACT fact, not a missing witness of the kill.\"*  Correct --\nthe round witnesses the TERMINATION, which is what it fixes; it does not claim to witness delivery,\nwhich it does not add.  The witness term stays `probe:`.\n\n**(4) MY §C0 COUNT WAS STALE** -- see `pass1_20260908/CORRECTION_C0.md`, written before this.  grok's\nframing adds the general rule: *\"Do not treat a DIRTY-TREE `grep -c` as the ship check.\"*\n\n*** (5) THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A BROKEN ROUND, AND IT NEEDS MEASUREMENT NOT READING. ***\n\ngrok: *\"The UART probe's `stub named` row is HOSTILE to member 3.  It wants `lca_interrupt_assert`\nABSENT.  A latched `fatal(\"...TODO\")` keeps that row RED FOREVER.  Drop or re-pin it under the\noracle-correction rule; do not ship member 3 against that want.\"*\n\n**The row is exactly as described:**\n`row(\"stub named\", \"no\" if \"lca_interrupt_assert\" not in t3 else \"yes\", \"no\", ...)`\n\n**BUT THE HAZARD MAY NOT REACH MY IMPLEMENTATION, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE MESSAGE TEXT.**  grok\nreasoned from a generic *latched `fatal(\"...TODO\")`* -- keeping the original wording, which contains\nthe function name.  The announce I wrote says *\"[ lca: ISA interrupt assert not implemented (Alpha\nhas no interrupt delivery); ignored  (once) ]\"* -- **\"ISA interrupt assert\", not\n`lca_interrupt_assert`** -- so the forbidden literal may never appear.\n\n> *** THAT IS A READING OF MY OWN STRING AND IT IS EXACTLY THE KIND OF CLAIM THIS PROJECT REQUIRES\n> TO BE MEASURED.  The post-fix run settles it; nothing is claimed here. ***  And whichever way it\n> lands, grok has identified a REAL DESIGN CONSTRAINT that was not in my brief and that I satisfied\n> by accident rather than by intent: **the announce must not contain the literal\n> `lca_interrupt_assert`, or it pins a shipped witness row red forever.**\n\n*** THE FIX IS IMPLEMENTED AND MEASURED, 2026-09-08.  ONE INSTRUMENT, BOTH DIRECTIONS. ***\n\n                                     PRE-FIX   POST-FIX\n    lca_irqstub_probe.py               3/6       6/6\n    lca_irqstub_uart_probe.py          2/5       5/5\n\nScratch clone `/tmp/gxlca`, baseline `git show HEAD:src/devices/dev_lca.c` rebuilt in the clone.\nNo project tree mutated.  Raws + a README naming which instrument produced which figure:\n`regress/artifacts/lcairqstubkill/fix_20260908/`.\n\n**THE DIFF:** both stubs keep a diagnostic and lose the termination -- option-set member 3, DECLINE\nplus a bounded announce.  **PER-INSTANCE** via `interrupt->extra`, not function-static, so one guest\naccess to one device instance cannot silence every other instance ([[staticlatchscope]]).  **TWO\nseparate latch bits**, because grok established the two callbacks are reached on DIFFERENT EDGES --\n`isa_interrupt_common` takes one edge per call -- so a shared bit would report whichever arrived\nfirst and hide the other entirely.\n\n**THREE ROWS ADDED, because both probes' own contracts demanded them.**  `lca_irqstub_probe.py`'s\nheader says: *\"it is NOT a detector of a repair: 'host survives' would green for a silent return, for\na latched announce, and for an actual interrupt implementation alike.  A fix round owes a row that\ndiscriminates those.\"*\n\n    irq announce   the announce text is present -- a silent return prints nothing, and a REAL\n                   implementation would print nothing either, so only this repair prints this\n    irq honest     the announce contains \"Alpha has no\" -- the round's claim is that it stops the\n                   host dying WITHOUT making interrupts work, and this is the only place a reader\n                   ever learns that\n    irq latched    COUNTS the announce in the free-running UART session, want exactly 1.  That\n                   session's tick re-asserts repeatedly, so an unlatched announce prints many times.\n                   Counting is the oracle because a PRESENCE row would be satisfied by a latch that\n                   never engages -- the very mutant it exists to catch.\n\n*** A DEFECT I INTRODUCED WHILE ADDING THEM, AND CAUGHT BY THE PROBE'S OWN OUTPUT. ***  The rows went\nin BELOW each score computation, splitting both scorers: numerator over the old row set, denominator\nevaluated at print time.  **The 8042 probe printed SIX ROWS ALL `ok` UNDER A SUMMARY OF 4/6.**  The\nUART probe reported 3/5 pre-fix where the honest score was 2/5 -- and post-fix it rendered 5/5\nCORRECTLY BY COINCIDENCE, because nothing failed.  Repaired in both files by moving the computation\nadjacent to the print.  A census over HEAD shows **0 of 73** candidate probes were ever split, so\nboth instances were mine; filed as [[splitscorer]] (low) with that correction.\n\n**KNOWN GAP, DISCLOSED IN THE PROBE UNDER CLAUSE (e): `lca_interrupt_deassert` IS WHOLLY\nUNWITNESSED.**  Neither witness reaches the deassert edge -- the 8042 probe never reads the `0x55`\nreply, and the UART probe leaves IER on and free-runs, so the aggregate never falls back to zero.\nIts latch bit and its whole body are uncovered and no mutant of them could be killed.  Closing it\nneeds a guest sequence that LOWERS the interrupt, which is a new session rather than a new row.  Put\nto pass 2 as an explicit question rather than shipped silently.\n\n*** PASS 2: grok returned NOT RATIFY, AND A MUTANT CONFIRMED IT.  My best row was vacuous. ***\n\n**Its verdict:** *\"The disposition in `dev_lca.c` is the right one.  The 11-row instrument does not\ndefend it.  Two floor holes, both from ONE false premise: that UART ticks re-enter\n`lca_interrupt_assert`.\"*\n\n**IT READ THE PATH INSTEAD OF THE BRIEF:** *\"`isa_interrupt_common` is EDGE-TRIGGERED on the PIC\naggregate.  If the UART never drops IRR, `irq latched` is vacuous.\"*\n\n*** MEASURED, NOT CONCEDED.  Mutant `M1_unlatch` removes the latch so the announce fires on EVERY\nentry: ***\n\n    base           kbc=6/6   uart=5/5   announce_count=1\n    M1_unlatch     kbc=6/6   uart=5/5   announce_count=1     *** CLEAN SWEEP ***\n\n**The count is 1 with and without the latch, because the stub is entered exactly ONCE however long\nthe guest free-runs.**  My `irq latched` row could not vary under the only mutant it existed to\ncatch.\n\n> **THIS IS THE CHANNEL FAILURE FOR THE FOURTH TIME IN TWO ROUNDS, AND THE SAME MISTAKE EVERY TIME:\n> I named an observable by asking what the code prints on the path I HAD IN MIND, never by tracing\n> what the MUTANT actually reaches.**  Here I assumed re-entry; the caller is edge-triggered.\n\n**DISPOSITION -- RELABELLED, NOT DELETED, and the distinction is the honest part.**  The row is\nvacuous *as a latch test* and is NOT vacuous *as a statement about behaviour*: the announce really is\nemitted once, and pinning that catches a future edit moving it outside the guard.  It is now\n`irq once`, its note says it does NOT test the latch, and the measurement is quoted beside it.\n\n*** AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS A GAP THIS ROUND CANNOT CLOSE: THE LATCH IS UNEXERCISED BY ANY WITNESS\nIN THIS TREE. ***  Exercising it needs TWO assert edges in one session -- an IRR that falls and rises\nagain -- which is the SAME missing capability as the unwitnessed deassert path.  So the round ships\na latch it cannot test, and says so under clause (e) rather than implying coverage.\n\n*** A SECOND CORRECTION FROM grok, and it was about to ship in PRODUCT CODE. ***  My comment said\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` delivers *\"for ARM, M88K and PPC only\"*.  Checked: it delivers for **FIVE** --\nARM (`:208`), M88K (`:212`), MIPS via the COP0 exception model (`:219`), PPC (`:243`) and SH via\n`int_to_assert` (`:247`).  **I had enumerated by grepping `irq_asserted`, which is a CORRELATE of\ndelivering rather than the property**, and missed the two that deliver by another mechanism.  *The\nfourth correlate-not-the-property error of the day.*  **The Alpha conclusion is unchanged and\nSTRENGTHENED -- it is the outlier among five, not three** -- and the comment now says so with the\nreason, because a wrong reason gets reused.\n\n**grok also CONFIRMED what it could:** P4 holds (two writes of `cd.alpha.irq_asserted`, no reads, no\n`DYNTRANS_ALPHA` arm); `interrupt->extra` is a `struct lca_data *` so forwarding is chipset wiring;\nand my Q1 reasoning -- the predicates that gated death are the EMPTY SET, because the stubs had no\n`if` at all -- is right.\n\n**AND IT WAS EXPLICIT ABOUT WHAT IT DID NOT DO:** *\"The scores 6/6 and 5/5 -- I did not re-run the\nprobes.  I read the artifacts.  Those logs match the brief.  They do NOT show an unlatch mutant.\"*\nThat last sentence is the whole finding, stated as a gap in the evidence rather than as an\naccusation, and it was correct.\n\n**FIX SHIPPED 2026-09-09 as `#516` (74daefd)**: both stubs de-fatalised, 12-row detector, tables G+H (16 cells, 14 killed, 2 measured equivalent), chain closed on pass 3.  State `in work` until the regress phase runs on it.",
    "hold_question": "`lcairqstubkill` IS NOW UNBLOCKED AND I WANT THE DISPOSITION RATIFIED OR REFUSED BEFORE IT SHIPS.  MEASURED: `cpu->cd.alpha.irq_asserted` is WRITTEN at cpu_alpha.c:218/223 and READ NOWHERE -- `cpu_dyntrans.c` dispatches interrupts for arm (:208), m88k (:212, :382) and ppc (:243), and Alpha is absent.  So the Alpha CPU model has an interrupt-pending flag with NO CONSUMER, and wiring the LCA stubs to `cpu_interrupt()` would set a field nothing reads.  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) Given forwarding is a measured NO-OP, is the right disposition a bounded (once) announce plus a plain return -- member 3 -- with an explicit disclosure that Alpha delivery does not exist?  Or should the round ALSO write `irq_asserted` even though nothing reads it, on the ground that a future Alpha dispatch would then find the state already correct?  I lean AGAINST that: it is the ADDITION LICENCE's 'cannot hurt' shape, and it would ship a line whose only justification is a subsystem nobody has scheduled.  (2) Does removing a host termination without adding delivery need its own SEVERITY restatement in the round block?  The fix buys 'the host stops dying', NOT 'interrupts start working', and I want to be sure the record cannot be read as the latter.  (3) THE WITNESS QUESTION: this row has TWO rung-3 witnesses on independent paths (8042 command port 3/4; ns16550 IRQ 4 2/4).  Both prove the termination.  NEITHER can prove the post-fix behaviour is correct, because 'the host survived and nothing happened' is indistinguishable from 'the host survived and an interrupt was delivered' WHEN NO DELIVERY MECHANISM EXISTS.  Is a detector even constructible here beyond survival plus the latch's own announce, or is this a case where `detector:` is the honest witness term and the contract must say the delivery half is unobservable by construction?"
